e same period's beetles to determine the temperature.\n\nC) The method of determining the date of beetles is more accurate than that of other organisms. The context doesn't mention anything about the accuracy of the dating method. It just says they determined their dates. So, this might not be an assumption.\n\nD) In the past 22,000 years, the tolerable temperature of beetles has not changed significantly. This seems relevant. If beetles' tolerable temperatures have changed over time, then using current data might not accurately reflect past conditions. The researcher needs to assume that beetles' temperature ranges haven't changed much, so their data is applicable.\n\nWait, but the question is about the assumption that the discussion depends on. The researcher is using beetles' tolerances to determine temperatures. So, the assumption is that beetles' tolerances haven't changed over the 22,000 years, otherwise, the data would be unreliable.\n\nSo, the correct answer should be D.\n"]
