n\nC) There are no birdlike dinosaur fossils that are older than the bird fossils but have not yet been unearthed. This seems relevant because the argument is based on the current fossil record. If there were older bird-like dinosaur fossils, it would support the argument. But the argument is saying that the bird fossils are older than the earliest bird-like dinosaur fossils, so the argument assumes that no such undiscovered fossils exist. So this could be the assumption.\n\nD) It could not have been the case that some birds were descendants from one bird-like dinosaur species and others from another. This seems like a possibility, but I'm not sure. The argument is about the earliest bird-like dinosaur, so maybe this isn't directly related.\n\nWait, let me think again. The argument is that because bird fossils are older than the earliest bird-like dinosaur fossils, the bird-like dinosaurs can't be the ancestors. So the argument assumes that there are no other bird-like dinosaurs that are even older than the ones found. If there were, then the argument would be weaker. So option C is saying that no such fossils exist, which is the assumption.\n\nOption B talks about not sharing features with other species, but the argument is specifically about the shared features with the earliest bird-like dinosaurs. So B might not be the right assumption.\n\nOption D is about whether birds could have come from different dinosaur species. The argument doesn't address that; it's more about the timing of the fossils. So D might not be the assumption.\n\nSo, putting it all together, the argument relies on the assumption that there are no older bird-like dinosaur fossils that have yet to be found. That's option C.\n"]
