So the statement is used to highlight that therapeutic success is the key point, making it the most important aspect. So B could be a possibility.\n\nC) It overrides some considerations against these theories. The critic is saying that even though the theories are poor in one way, they're better in another. So the therapeutic success is used to counter the argument that the theories are poor. So C might also be a possibility.\n\nD) It disproves evidence against these theories. I'm not sure. The statement is about therapeutic success, which is evidence in favor of the theories, but I don't think it's disproving anything. It's more about supporting the point that despite their flaws, they're effective.\n\nSo between B and C. Let me think again. The statement is used to argue that therapeutic success is more important than the theories being scientific. So it's highlighting that aspect as the most important, which would make B correct. Alternatively, it's countering the criticism that the theories are poor because they're inelegant, so it's overriding that consideration, making C correct.\n\nWait, the critic is saying that the theories are poor in one way but successful in another. So the therapeutic success is used to counter the criticism that they're poor. So it's overriding the consideration that their inelegance and lack of explaining the psyche makes them bad theories. So C might be the answer.\n\nBut I'm a bit confused because B says it's used to illustrate the most important aspect of scientific theories. But the critic isn't saying that scientific theories are important; they're saying that therapeutic success is more important. So maybe B is not correct.\n\nAlternatively, the statement is used to show that despite the theories being poor, they have more success, so it's countering the initial point about them being poor. So C seems more accurate.\n\nWait, but the options are about the role of the statement in the critic's argument. The critic is saying that the theories are poor but have more success. So the statement about therapeutic success is used to argue that despite their flaws, they're better in another aspect, which is more important. So it's overriding the consideration that their poor aspects make them bad theories.\n\nSo I think C is the correct answer because the statement is used to override the consideration against these theories, countering the initial point that they're poor.\n</think>\n\nThe statement about therapeutic success is used to override some considerations against these theories. \n\nAnswer: C",
