, C, D.\n\nLet me break down the context. The EU needs Turkey for cooperation in various areas, but joining comes with challenges. So, the EU can't have both everything they want and avoid all problems. That seems to point towards option A.\n\nOption B says Turkey can join if it meets the EU's criteria. But the context doesn't say that Turkey has met the criteria or that the EU will accept it. It just mentions that the EU needs Turkey but the process is difficult. So B might not be correct.\n\nOption C suggests the EU can either get full cooperation or avoid the problems. But the context says that if Turkey joins, it brings problems. So the EU can't avoid the problems if they accept Turkey. So C doesn't seem right.\n\nOption D talks about the percentage of Turkey in Europe and recognition. The context doesn't mention anything about Turkey's population or recognition status. So D is probably not correct.\n\nSo, putting it all together, the main point is that the EU can't have both full cooperation and avoid all problems. That's exactly what option A says. So I think A is the correct answer.\n"]
