 tail of integrin and activate integrin." The context says Hsp90 binds to integrins, but it doesn\'t specify where on the integrin. The option mentions the "tail" of integrin, which isn\'t mentioned in the context. So, this might not be accurate. I\'m leaning towards this being incorrect because the context doesn\'t specify the exact binding site.\n\nOption C: "Not only fever, other pressure can also induce the expression of Hsp90 in T lymphocytes." The context only talks about fever inducing Hsp90 expression. There\'s no mention of other pressures, so this option is speculative. It might be true, but since the context doesn\'t support it, it\'s not the best answer.\n\nOption D: "After Hsp90 binds to integrin, it can activate the signaling pathway that promotes the migration of T lymphocytes." This directly relates to the context. The context explains that Hsp90 binds to integrins, which then leads to the migration of T cells. So, this option is a clear support of the discussion because it follows the cause-effect chain described.\n\nSo, comparing all options, Option D directly supports the explanation given in the context by linking the binding of Hsp90 to integrins with the signaling pathway that promotes migration. Option A is also somewhat correct but doesn\'t tie into the specific mechanism involving Hsp90 and integrins. Option B is incorrect because it adds a detail not mentioned, and Option C is speculative without evidence from the context.\n\nTherefore, the best answer is Option D.\n']
