e\'s saying that because the chance is high, the statement is false, which is treating probability as certainty.\n\nSo, in that case, option A would be the error. Because he\'s using the probability as if it\'s a certainty, which is a common logical error.\n\nWait, but option A says that statements that absolutely establish the conclusion are treated as if they merely support it. So if the conclusion is not absolutely established, but only supported by probability, then maybe A is not the error.\n\nI\'m getting stuck here. Let me try to think of each option:\n\nA) Statements that absolutely establish the conclusion are treated as if they merely support it. So if Dobson had a statement that absolutely established the conclusion, he would treat it as supporting, not as an absolute. But in this case, he\'s using probability, not an absolute statement. So maybe A is not the error.\n\nB) Failure of cited evidence to establish a statement is taken as evidence the statement is false. But Dobson is using the cited evidence (two stones) to support his conclusion, not as evidence against it. So B might not be the error.\n\nC) Something that\'s merely opinion is treated as if subject to verification. Not relevant here.\n\nD) Conclusion contradicts some evidence. No, the evidence is used to support the conclusion, not contradict it.\n\nWait, maybe I\'m misunderstanding the options. Let me read them again.\n\nOption B: The failure of cited evidence to establish a statement is taken as evidence that the statement is false.\n\nBut in Dobson\'s argument, the cited evidence (two stones pointing) is used to support the statement (they were knowledgeable). So he\'s not failing the evidence; he\'s using it. So B doesn\'t apply.\n\nOption A: Statements that absolutely establish the conclusion are treated as if they merely support it. But Dobson\'s conclusion isn\'t absolutely established; he\'s arguing against it using probability. So maybe A is the error because he\'s treating the prob