final_output:  ['\n\nThe correct answer is D) One member happens to be a member of the two sub-committees.\n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**\n\n1. **Understanding the Problem:**\n   - There are 6 members: F, G, H, I, M, P.\n   - Each member serves in at least one of three sub-committees.\n   - Each sub-committee has three members, totaling 9 member slots across three sub-committees.\n   - One member serves in all three sub-committees.\n   - F and G cannot be in the same sub-committee.\n   - H and I cannot be in the same sub-committee.\n\n2. **Analyzing the Constraints:**\n   - Since one member is in all three sub-committees, they contribute two extra slots (as they are already counted once in each sub-committee).\n   - The remaining two extra slots must come from two other members, each serving in two sub-committees.\n\n3. **Evaluating the Options:**\n   - **Option A:** If F or G were in all three, they would force the other to be in none, which is impossible. Thus, A is not necessarily true.\n   - **Option B:** Similarly, if H or I were in all three, the other would have to be in none, which is impossible. Thus, B is not necessarily true.\n   - **Option C:** If M is the one in all three, P could be in two or three sub-committees, so C is not necessarily true.\n   - **Option D:** The member in all three sub-committees is in two sub-committees in addition to the third, making them a member of two sub-committees. Thus, D must be true.\n\n**Answer: D**', '\nOkay, so I\'m trying to solve this logical reasoning problem. Let me read through it again to make sure I understand all the details.\n\nWe have a Comprehensive Management Committee with 6 members: F, G, H, I, M, and P. Each of these members must serve on at least one of the three sub-committees. Each sub-committee has three different members. So, there are three sub-committees, each with three members, making a total of 9 member positions across all sub-committees. But since there are only 6 members, some must serve