wo more than one. But the option says "only one," so that\'s not true. Wait, no, the option says "P or M is a member of only one sub-committee." But M is in three, so M is not in only one. So, perhaps P is in only one.\n\nBut let\'s see. If M is in all three, then P could be in only one, but it\'s not necessarily the case. Let me think.\n\nWait, let\'s try to construct a scenario where P is in only one sub-committee.\n\nIf M is in all three, then P could be in only one, but maybe not necessarily. Let me see.\n\nAlternatively, maybe P is in two sub-committees, and M is in three.\n\nWait, but the question is whether C must be true. So, if in all possible scenarios, either P or M is in only one sub-committee, then C is true.\n\nBut let\'s see. If M is in all three, then M is in three, so not only one. So, if P is in only one, then C is true. But is it necessarily the case?\n\nWait, maybe not. Let me try to see.\n\nSuppose M is in all three, and P is in two sub-committees. Then, P is not in only one, so C would be false because neither P nor M is in only one. Wait, but M is in three, so C says "P or M is a member of only one sub-committee." If M is in three, then M is not in only one, so P must be in only one. But is that necessarily the case?\n\nWait, no, because P could be in two or three. So, maybe C is not necessarily true.\n\nWait, but let\'s think again. If M is in all three, then P could be in only one, but it\'s not necessarily the case. So, maybe C is not necessarily true.\n\nOption D: One member happens to be a member of two sub-committees.\n\nWell, we know that one member is in all three, so that member is in two sub-committees in addition to the third, so yes, that member is in two sub-committees. So, D is true because that member is in two sub-committees.\n\nWait, but the question is asking which must be true. So, D is true because the member in all three is in two sub-committees. But let me check the options again.\n\nWait, the options are:\n\nA) One of F 