m the following 4 judges: F, G, H, I, and 2 from the following 3 scientists: V, Y, Z. So, each year, 2 judges and 2 scientists.\n\nWait, but the initial statement says \"2 of which are from the following 4 judges: F, G, H, I, and the other 2 members are from the following 3 scientists: V, Y, Z.\" So, each year, 2 judges (from F, G, H, I) and 2 scientists (from V, Y, Z).\n\nSo, in Year 1, H is the chairman, so he's a judge. Therefore, the other judge in Year 1 could be F, G, or I. The two scientists could be any combination of V, Y, Z, but with the constraints.\n\nNow, let's apply the constraints for Year 1.\n\nConstraint 3: G and V cannot be in the same year. So, if G is in Year 1, V cannot be in Year 1, and vice versa.\n\nConstraint 4: H and Y cannot be in the same year. But H is in Year 1, so Y cannot be in Year 1. Therefore, in Year 1, the two scientists cannot include Y. So, the scientists in Year 1 must be V and Z, or Y and Z, but wait, Y cannot be in Year 1 because H is chairman. So, Y is excluded. Therefore, the scientists in Year 1 must be V and Z, because Y is not allowed.\n\nWait, but constraint 3 says G and V cannot be in the same year. So, if V is in Year 1, G cannot be in Year 1. So, in Year 1, if we have V as a scientist, then the judges cannot include G. But H is already a judge in Year 1, so the other judge must be F or I.\n\nWait, let's try to outline Year 1.\n\nYear 1:\n\nChair: H (judge)\n\nJudges: H and either F, G, or I. But since H is already a judge, the other judge must be one of F, G, I.\n\nScientists: Since Y cannot be in Year 1 (because H is chairman), the scientists must be V and Z, or V and Y, but Y is excluded, so only V and Z.\n\nBut constraint 3: G and V cannot be in the same year. So, if V is a scientist in Year 1, then G cannot be a judge in Year 1.\n\nSo, if V is a scientist in Year 1, then the judges are H and either F or I (since G is excluded).\n\nAlternatively, if V is not in Year 1, then the scientists would be Y and Z, but Y cannot be in Year 1 because H is chairman, so that's not possible. Therefore, V must be in Year 1.\n\nSo, Year 1 must include V as a scientist, and thus G cannot be a judge in Year 1.\n\nTherefore, Year 1's judges are H and either F or I.\n\nSo, Year 1's committee is:\n\nChair: H\n\nJudges: H and either F or I\n\nScientists: V and Z\n\nSo, possible Year 1 committees:\n\nOption 1: H, F, V, Z\n\nOption 2: H, I, V, Z\n\nNow, moving to Year 2.\n\nYear 2:\n\nChair: Must be a member of Year 1's committee. So, the members of Year 1 are H, F/I, V, Z. So, possible chairs for Year 2: H, F/I, V, Z.\n\nBut H is the chairman of Year 1, so according to rule 1, H must retire during Year 2, meaning H cannot be the chairman of Year 2.\n\nTherefore, Year 2's chairman must be either F, I, V, or Z.\n\nBut also, the chairman of Year 2 must be a member of Year 1's committee, which includes F, I, V, Z.\n\nSo, possible options for Year 2's chairman: F, I, V, Z.\n\nBut we also have to consider the constraints for Year 2.\n\nConstraints for Year 2:\n\nConstraint 3: G and V cannot be in the same year. But G is not in Year 2 because in Year 1, G was excluded because V was included. So, G is not in Year 2, so this constraint doesn't affect Year 2.\n\nConstraint 4: H and Y cannot be in the same year. But H is not in Year 2, so this doesn't affect Year 2.\n\nConstraint 5: Each year, only one of I and V is a member.\n\nSo, in Year 2, only one of I or V can be a member.\n\nBut wait, Year 2's committee must have 2 judges and 2 scientists.\n\nLet's think about Year 2's possible committees.\n\nFirst, Year 2's chairman must be F, I, V, or Z.\n\nBut also, Year 2's committee must include only one of I or V.\n\nSo, let's consider each possible chairman and see if it's possible.\n\nOption 1: Year 2's chairman is F.\n\nIf F is the chairman, then F is a judge. So, the other judge must be someone else. But in Year 1, the judges were H and either F or I. If Year 1's judges were H and F, then in Year 2, the oth