veryone, so everyone must have answered. Therefore, Xiao Wang's calls were answered by everyone, meaning that Xiao Wang answered the calls from others. Wait, no, Xiao Wang called them, but whether he answered depends on whether he received a call. The context doesn't say that Xiao Wang received any calls. So, it's possible that Xiao Wang didn't answer any calls, but he called everyone. So, the fact that he called everyone doesn't necessarily mean he answered them. Therefore, option D is incorrect.\n\nWait, I'm getting confused. Let me clarify:\n\n- Xiao Wang called everyone (dialled their phones). So, he sent calls to them, but it's not specified whether he received any calls.\n\n- The third point says that if someone hasn't answered calls from others, they haven't called anyone else. So, if someone didn't answer any calls, they also didn't call anyone else.\n\n- So, if Xiao Wang called everyone, then everyone must have answered because if they hadn't answered, they wouldn't have called anyone else. But Xiao Wang called everyone, so everyone must have answered. Therefore, Xiao Wang's calls were answered by everyone, meaning that Xiao Wang answered the calls from others. Wait, no, Xiao Wang called them, but whether he answered depends on whether he received a call. The context doesn't say that Xiao Wang received any calls. So, it's possible that Xiao Wang didn't answer any calls, but he called everyone. Therefore, option D is incorrect because it's the other way around. The people dialed by Xiao Wang (i.e., others) might have answered or not, but Xiao Wang didn't necessarily answer.\n\nWait, no, the third point is about people who haven't answered calls from others. So, if someone didn't answer, they also didn't call anyone else. But Xiao Wang called everyone, so everyone must have answered because otherwise, they wouldn't have called anyone else. So, everyone must have answered Xiao Wang's calls. Therefore, Xiao Wang's calls were answered by everyone, meaning that Xiao Wang answered the calls from others. Wait, no, that's not correct. The third point is about people who haven't answered calls from others, not about Xiao Wang. So, if someone didn't answer, they also didn't call anyone else. But Xiao Wang called everyone, so everyone must have answered because if they hadn't answered, they wouldn't have called anyone else. Therefore, everyone answered Xiao Wang's calls, meaning that Xiao Wang's calls were answered by others. But option D says that Xiao Wang answered the phone from everyone, which would mean that Xiao Wang received calls and answered them. But the context doesn't say that Xiao Wang received any calls. So, we can't infer that. Therefore, option D is incorrect.\n\nSo, going back, option B is correct because Xiao Li called Xiao Zhao, and since Xiao Zhao didn't call anyone else, he must have answered the call from Xiao Wang. Therefore, Xiao Li must have answered Xiao Wang's call.\n\nWait, no, let me clarify again. Xiao Wang called everyone, including Xiao Li. So, Xiao Wang's call to Xiao Li must have been answered by Xiao Li because if Xiao Li hadn't answered, he wouldn't have called anyone else, but he did call Xiao Zhao. Therefore, Xiao Li must have answered Xiao Wang's call. So, option B is correct.\n\nYes, I think that's the correct reasoning.\n</think>\n\nThe correct answer is B) Xiao Li once answered a phone call from Xiao Wang.\n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**\n\n1. **Understanding the Context:**\n   - Xiao Wang called everyone in the department (everyone dialed their phones).\n   - Xiao Li called Xiao Zhao once, and Xiao Zhao never called anyone else.\n   - If someone hasn't answered calls from others, they also haven't called anyone else.\n\n2. **Analyzing Option B:**\n   - Xiao Wang called Xiao Li (since he called everyone).\n   - For Xiao Zhao to have never called anyone else, he must have answered Xiao Wang's call to Xiao Li.\n   - Therefore, Xiao Li must have answered Xiao Wang's call.\n\n3. **Other Opti