be higher than both -50\u00b0C and -85\u00b0C. But the question is about any point in the stratosphere. Since the stratosphere's temperature increases with altitude, any point higher than the top of the troposphere would have a higher temperature than the top of the troposphere. So, for any point in the stratosphere, the temperature would indeed be higher than the corresponding point in the troposphere directly beneath it. Therefore, B seems to be true.\n\nOption C: The temperature in the middle part of the stratosphere over the north pole is at least as great as the temperature in the middle part of the stratosphere over the equator. The context doesn't provide specific information about the temperatures in the middle of the stratosphere over these regions. It only mentions the temperatures at the top of the troposphere. Without knowing how the temperature increases in the stratosphere for each hemisphere, we can't determine if the middle part over the poles is warmer than the middle part over the equator. So, C might not necessarily be true.\n\nOption D: It is warmer at the top of the stratosphere over the poles than it is at the top of the stratosphere over the equator. The top of the stratosphere over the poles is warmer than the top of the stratosphere over the equator because the stratosphere's temperature increases with altitude, and the starting point (top of the troposphere) is warmer over the poles (-50\u00b0C) than over the equator (-85\u00b0C). So, the top of the stratosphere over the poles would indeed be warmer than the top over the equator. Therefore, D seems to be true.\n\nWait, but I need to make sure. Let me think again. The context says that the stratosphere's temperature increases as you go up, but it doesn't specify whether the rate of increase is the same in both hemispheres. However, since the starting point (top of the troposphere) is warmer over the poles, and both stratospheres start from their respective tops, the top of the stratosphere over the poles would be warmer than the top over the equator. So D is correct.\n\nBut wait, the question is asking which must be true. Both B and D seem to be correct based on the reasoning. Let me check the options again.\n\nWait, the options are A, B, C, D. The question is which one must be true. So, both B and D could be correct, but let me see if the options allow for multiple correct answers. No, it's a single choice. So perhaps I made a mistake.\n\nWait, let me re-examine option B. It says the temperature at any point in the stratosphere is at least as great as the temperature at the top of the troposphere directly beneath it. So, for example, at the top of the troposphere over the poles (-50\u00b0C), the stratosphere starts and increases from there. So, any point in the stratosphere above that would be warmer than -50\u00b0C. Similarly, over the equator, the stratosphere starts at -85\u00b0C, so any point above that would be warmer than -85\u00b0C. But the question is whether any point in the stratosphere is at least as warm as the temperature at the top of the troposphere directly beneath it. So, for a point in the stratosphere over the poles, it's warmer than -50\u00b0C, which is the temperature at the top of the troposphere there. Similarly, over the equator, any point in the stratosphere is warmer than -85\u00b0C. So, yes, B is correct.\n\nBut wait, the question is about any point in the stratosphere, not just the top. So, for example, if you take a point in the stratosphere over the poles, its temperature is higher than -50\u00b0C, which is the temperature at the top of the troposphere there. Similarly, over the equator, any point in the stratosphere is higher than -85\u00b0C. So, B is correct.\n\nBut then D is also correct because the top of the stratosphere over the poles is warmer than the top over the equator. So, both B and D seem correct. But looking back at the options, perhaps I misread something.\n\nWait, the question is asking which one must als