at, if accurate, would support the main conclusion, and the second is a conclusion drawn to support the main conclusion. That makes sense because the first part is a prediction about middle school results, which if true, would lower the CEHE ranking, supporting the conclusion. The second part is the conclusion itself.\n\nOption D says the first is a prediction supporting the main conclusion, and the second is the main conclusion. That\'s also possible, but the question is about the roles of the two bolded parts. The first is a prediction, and the second is the conclusion drawn from it.\n\nWait, but in the argument, the main conclusion is that Silver County will establish a new low. The first bolded part is a prediction about middle school results, which if accurate, would support that conclusion. So the first is a prediction supporting the conclusion, and the second is the conclusion itself. So D seems correct.\n\nBut let me double-check. The first bolded statement is presented as a prediction, and the argument concludes that the CEHE ranking will be lower. So the first is a prediction that supports the conclusion, and the second is the conclusion. So D is correct.\n\nI think I\'m confident with D.\n'] 10%|█         | 60/600 [44:22<5:31:58, 36.89s/it]
