 students are not children of migrant workers.\n\nThe context says that some first-grade students are children of migrant workers, but it doesn't say anything about the rest. It's possible that all first graders are migrant children, or maybe some aren't. We don't have enough information to conclude this. So D might not be correct.\n\nWait, let me double-check. The context says \"some first-grade students are the children of migrant workers,\" which means at least one is. But it doesn't say anything about the others. So we can't conclude that some aren't. So D is not necessarily true.\n\nSo, the only conclusion we can definitively make is option C: Some students with household registration in this city are third-grade students.\n</think>\n\nThe correct answer is C.\n\n**Answer: C) Some students with household registration in this city are third-grade students.**\n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**\n\n1. **Understanding the Context:**\n   - All third-grade students are registered in the city.\n   - Some second-grade students are registered.\n   - Some first-grade students are children of migrant workers.\n   - These migrant children are not registered in the city.\n\n2. **Analyzing Each Option:**\n   - **Option A:** The context doesn't specify whether all second graders are or aren't migrant children. It only mentions some are registered, so we can't conclude all are not.\n   - **Option B:** There's no information about third graders being migrant children, so we can't conclude this.\n   - **Option C:** Since all third graders are registered, some registered students must be third graders. This is a direct conclusion.\n   - **Option D:** The context only states some first graders are migrant children, not that some aren't. So we can't conclude this.\n\n3. **Conclusion:**\n   - Only option C is supported by the given information.\n\n**Answer:** C",
