 prediction.\n\nC) Apex will use funds from the sale of five casinos to help fund its acquisition of Eldorado.\n- This seems like a positive point. It shows that selling five casinos will help with acquiring Eldorado, which is necessary. So this supports the prediction, not undermines it. So probably not the answer.\n\nD) Some of the state's counties do not permit casinos.\n- If some counties don't allow casinos, then when Apex sells five casinos, maybe those five are in counties that don't permit them. So selling in those counties might not be possible, meaning they can't sell five. That would mean they can't get Eldorado, so their plan fails. This would definitely undermine the prediction because they can't sell the required five casinos.\n\nSo between B and D. Option B says that only three organizations are licensed, so when Apex sells five, maybe they can't find a buyer because there's no one else. But actually, if only three are licensed, and Apex is selling five, but they only have twenty left, maybe they can sell to Moneyland? Wait, but Moneyland already has many casinos. Maybe they can't sell to Moneyland because Moneyland is already operating in many counties. So if Moneyland is already in most counties, Apex might not be able to sell to them because they might not need more. Alternatively, if Moneyland is the only other licensed organization, then Apex could sell to them, but if they're already in many counties, maybe they can't sell more because Moneyland is saturated.\n\nWait, but the context says that Moneyland is already the most casinos in the state, so maybe they can't take on more. But the question is about whether the prediction is undermined. If Apex can't sell because Moneyland is already saturated, then they can't get the five casinos, so their plan fails. That would undermine the prediction.\n\nAlternatively, option D says some counties don't permit casinos. So when Apex tries to sell five casinos, maybe those five are in counties that don't al