      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out this logical reasoning problem. Let me read it carefully.\n\nThe context says: \"Obligatory alternative action refers to a situation in which the actor has committed an illegal act and caused the result, but the result cannot be avoided even if he abides by the law.\"\n\nSo, the key points here are:\n1. The actor commits an illegal act.\n2. This causes a result.\n3. Even if the actor follows the law, the result can't be avoided.\n\nThe question is asking which of the options is an \"optional act of obligation.\" Hmm, I think that means which scenario fits the definition of obligatory alternative action.\n\nLet me break down the options one by one.\n\nOption A: A was driving normally, a pedestrian named Wang rushed to A from the sidewalk and was injured. So, A was driving normally, which means A didn't commit an illegal act. The pedestrian was the one rushing, causing the accident. So, this doesn't fit because the actor (A) wasn't committing an illegal act.\n\nOption B: B was drunk driving, pedestrian Wang also drunk, rushed to B from the sidewalk, injured. Here, B is the one driving, and he's drunk, which is illegal. The pedestrian is also drunk, but the question is whether the result (injury) can be avoided if B abided by the law. If B drove normally, would the pedestrian still have rushed and caused the accident? I think so, because the pedestrian's intoxication might be contributing, but the main point is that B's illegal act (drunken driving) caused the result, and even if B followed the law, the pedestrian's action might still have led to the accident. So this seems to fit.\n\nOption C: C was driving speeding, pedestrian Wang across the road, C too late to brake, hit. So, C was speeding, which is illegal. The pedestrian was across the road, and C didn't have time to brake. If C had followed the law (not speeding), would the pedestrian still have been across the road? Maybe, but the question is whether the result can be avoided even if C abided by the law. If C drove normally, the pedestrian might have still been across the road, so the accident would still happen. So this also fits the definition.\n\nOption D: D was overloaded driving, pedestrian Wang across the road, D had emergency brake, didn't hit. So, D was overloaded, which is illegal. The pedestrian was across the road, but D had an emergency brake, so he avoided the accident. If D had followed the law (not overloaded), would he have had an emergency brake? Probably not, so the accident wouldn't have happened. Therefore, the result can be avoided if D abided by the law, so this doesn't fit the definition.\n\nWait, but the question is about \"optional act of obligation.\" I think that refers to the actor's action being optional, meaning they could have chosen a different action. But in the context, it's about the actor committing an illegal act. So, maybe I'm mixing up the terms.\n\nWait, the definition is about the actor committing an illegal act and causing a result that can't be avoided even if they followed the law. So, the key is that the result is unavoidable regardless of their lawful actions.\n\nSo, in Option B, B was drunk driving, which is illegal. The pedestrian was also drunk, so maybe the result is unavoidable because both were drunk. If B followed the law, he wouldn't have been driving drunk, but the pedestrian's intoxication might still cause the accident. So, the result can't be avoided even if B followed the law.\n\nIn Option C, C was speeding, which is illegal. The pedestrian was across the road, so if C drove normally, the pedestrian might still have been across the road, leading to the accident. So, the result is unavoidable even if C followed the law.\n\nIn Option D, D was overloaded, which is illegal, but he had an emergency brake, so the accident was avoided. If D followed the law, he wouldn't have had an emergency brake, so the accident could have been avoided. So, this doesn't fit.\n\