s. But it doesn't directly question the manufacturers' optimism about the market size.\n\nB) China's bicycle ownership is about half the world's, at least 470 million. This is a strong statement about the market size. If this is correct, it supports the manufacturers' view that the market is huge. So, this doesn't question their optimism; it actually reinforces it.\n\nC) Green awareness is increasing, making cycling a better choice. This is a positive factor for the manufacturers, as more people might buy bikes. Again, it supports their optimism rather than questioning it.\n\nD) Foreign brands are expensive in China and exceed ordinary people's affordability. This directly challenges the manufacturers' assumption that the market is affordable. If foreign bikes are too expensive, they might not be able to sell well, which would question their optimism about entering the market.\n\nSo, the best option to question the manufacturers' views is D because it directly contradicts their assumption about the market's affordability.\n"]
