two styles but doesn't consider if both are appropriate for the same audience. So, maybe politicians are using the wrong style for their audience, which could be a problem.\n\nI'm a bit torn between C and D. Let me think again.\n\nThe argument is saying that politicians should adopt the scholars' approach because it's more persuasive. But if it's difficult to come up with these formulations, then politicians might not be able to do it. So, the argument doesn't address the difficulty, making it vulnerable. That's option C.\n\nAlternatively, option D is about the styles being suited to similar audiences. The argument is comparing two styles but doesn't say whether both are suitable for the same group. Maybe politicians are using the wrong style for their audience, which could be a problem. But the argument doesn't specify that, so it's a bit more general.\n\nI think the main issue is that the argument doesn't address the difficulty of formulating these positions, so option C is the best answer.\n"] 21%|██▏       | 128/600 [1:37:37<3:20:28, 25.48s/it]
