eep is necessary.\n\nOption C: The participants who took a nap are all younger teenagers, and teenagers have better memory. So, if the nap group is teenagers, and they have better memory, maybe it's their age, not the sleep, that's causing the better performance. This could mean that the improvement is due to being teenagers, not necessarily because they slept. So this could refute the conclusion that sleep is the cause.\n\nOption D: Part of the nap group took the test relaxed and did well. This seems a bit redundant because the conclusion already mentions that the sleeping group did better. If a subset of the nap group did well, it doesn't necessarily contradict the conclusion. It might even support it, as it's part of the group that slept.\n\nNow, I need to decide which option best refutes the conclusion. The conclusion is that sleep helps store memory. So, if the results could be explained without needing sleep, that would refute it.\n\nOption B suggests that some participants who were told to take the test early didn't sleep but still did well because they unconsciously reviewed. This means that the test scores could be due to being told to take the test early, not necessarily because they slept. So this weakens the conclusion that sleep is the cause.\n\nOption C suggests that the nap group's age is the reason for better memory, not the sleep itself. So if the nap group is teenagers, maybe their better performance is because they're younger, not because they slept. This also weakens the conclusion.\n\nSo both B and C could potentially refute the conclusion. But which one is better?\n\nOption B directly shows that not all participants who took the test early performed well. It's a subset that didn't sleep but still did well. This could imply that the improvement isn't solely due to sleep.\n\nOption C points out that the nap group's age might be the reason for better memory, which could mean that the improvement is due to being teenagers, not necessarily sleep.\n\nI think both are valid, but perhaps B is a stronger refutation because it directly shows that the test scores could be due to being told to take the test early, not necessarily sleep. Whereas C is more about the age group, which might be a separate factor.\n\nWait, but the question is about the conclusion that sleep helps store memory. So if the nap group's performance is better because they're teenagers, that doesn't necessarily mean sleep isn't helpful. It could still be that sleep helps, but the age is another factor. So C might not fully refute the conclusion, just points out another variable.\n\nOn the other hand, B shows that some participants who were told to take the test early didn't sleep and still did well, suggesting that the test scores could be due to being told to take the test, not necessarily sleep. This directly challenges the conclusion that sleep is necessary for better memory storage.\n\nTherefore, I think B is the better option to refute the conclusion.\n"] 22%|██▏       | 130/600 [1:38:03<2:25:49, 18.62s/it] 22%|██▏       | 131/600 [1:38:07<1:49:58, 14.07s/it]
