ne pays the toll, they can use it, but if they don\'t pay, they can\'t. So, it\'s excludable because only those who pay can use it. Therefore, it\'s not a public good.\n\nOption D: Herdsman\'s meadows. This is a bit trickier. Herdsman\'s meadows are areas where a herdsman has control over grazing his animals. In some cases, these meadows are open to the public, allowing others to graze their own animals as well. Since they\'re not fenced off and can be used by anyone, it\'s non-excludable. So, it fits the definition of a public good.\n\nWait, but I\'m a bit confused about option B. Affordable housing can sometimes be public goods, like public housing projects, but the term "affordable" is broad. However, the question is asking which is a public good based on the definition, so I think the best fit is D because it\'s clearly non-excludable, whereas the others are excludable.\n']
