 becoming internal friction, leading to economic disputes or contradictions. So this seems to align with the context.\n\nD) The problem of labor income is the decisive factor of social stability.\n- The context doesn't specifically mention labor income. It talks about income gaps in general and social unrest. There's no information about labor income being the decisive factor. So D might not be correct.\n\nPutting it all together, option C seems to directly address the issue of unclear accounts leading to economic disputes, which is what the context is pointing towards. The other options either don't fit or are not supported by the context.\n</think>\n\nThe correct interpretation of the article is that economic disputes are often caused by unclear accounts.\n\n**Answer: C) Economic disputes are often caused by unclear accounts**",
