      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to solve this logical reasoning problem based on the given context. Let me read through the context again to make sure I understand it correctly.\n\nThe context says: \"The literary works of the Chinese writer Mo Yan have made the hidden Chinese vernacular concerned by the readers of the world and recognized by the international literary world, which has once again proved that this judgment is that only the national is the world.\"\n\nHmm, that's a bit dense. Let me break it down. It's talking about Mo Yan's literary works and how they've influenced both Chinese vernacular (which I think means everyday language or culture) and the international literary world. It mentions that this achievement proves that \"only the national is the world.\" \n\nSo, the key point here is that Mo Yan's work has shown that national identity is what connects to the world. In other words, the national aspect is what makes something (in this case, Mo Yan's work) recognized internationally.\n\nNow, looking at the question: Based on the above information, which of the following options is correct? The options are A, B, C, D.\n\nLet me look at each option one by one.\n\nOption A: \"It's national, it's world.\" This seems to suggest that if something is national, then it's also world. But I'm not sure if that's exactly what the context is saying. The context is more about how Mo Yan's national work is recognized internationally, so maybe this is a possible interpretation, but I'm not certain.\n\nOption B: \"If it is not national, it is not world.\" This is a bit tricky. It's a contrapositive of the statement \"Only national is world.\" In logic, \"Only P is Q\" translates to \"If not P, then not Q.\" So this seems to align with the context. If something isn't national, then it can't be world. That makes sense because Mo Yan's work is national and recognized internationally.\n\nOption C: \"As long as the nation is, it is the world.\" This seems similar to Option A. It's saying that nation implies world, which is somewhat true based on the context, but I think Option B is more precise because it's using the logical structure correctly.\n\nOption D: \"Only the world is national.\" This seems a bit off. The context is about national works being recognized as world, not the other way around. So it's not saying that only the world is national, but rather that national works are recognized as world.\n\nWait, let me think again. The context says that Mo Yan's work is both national and recognized internationally. So the connection is that being national leads to being recognized as world. So the correct logical statement would be that if something is national, then it's world. But in formal logic, \"only P is Q\" translates to \"If Q, then P,\" which is equivalent to \"Only P is Q.\" So the correct translation would be \"Only national is world,\" which is the same as saying \"If it's not national, then it's not world,\" which is Option B.\n\nSo, putting it all together, the correct answer should be Option B.\n</think>\n\nThe correct answer is B) If it is not national, it is not world.\n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**\n\n1. **Understanding the Context:** The context highlights that Mo Yan's literary works, being national, are recognized internationally. This establishes a connection between national identity and world recognition.\n\n2. **Analyzing the Options:**\n   - **Option A:** Suggests that being national implies being world, which is partially true but doesn't capture the logical structure fully.\n   - **Option B:** Uses the contrapositive of the statement, correctly translating \"Only national is world\" into \"If not national, then not world.\"\n   - **Option C:** Similar to A, it's somewhat true but less precise.\n   - **Option D:** Incorrectly reverses the relationship.\n\n3. **Logical Translation:** The context aligns with the logical statement \"Only P is Q,\" which translates to \"If not P, then not Q.\" Here, P is \"national\" and Q is \"world.\"\n\n4. **Conclusion:** Option B accurately reflects the logical structure of the given context.\n\n**Answer:** B) If it is not national, it is not world.",
