argument presents possible explanations (Mercury, Venus, Mars) and then systematically rules out the ones that don't fit, leaving only Mars as a viable option.\n\nLooking at the options:\n\nA) Eliminating competing alternative explanations – This fits because the argument rules out Mercury and Venus.\n\nB) Contrasting present circumstances with past circumstances – I don't think this applies here. The argument isn't comparing past events to present ones but rather eliminating options based on their properties.\n\nC) Abstracting a general principle from specific data – This doesn't seem right. The argument isn't creating a general rule but rather using specific information to eliminate options.\n\nD) Offering a counterexample to a theory – The argument isn't presenting a counterexample; it's using evidence to eliminate possibilities.\n\nSo, the correct answer should be A.\n"]
