n library users isn\'t solely because of the new libraries, or that there are other factors making the numbers look better.\n\nLooking at the options:\n\nA) The library needs to buy new books and has high maintenance costs, affecting other cultural facilities. Hmm, this is more about the cost and impact on other projects. It doesn\'t directly relate to the number of people using the libraries. So maybe not the strongest weakener.\n\nB) There are two universities, and many students applied for library cards for these three libraries. So if a lot of students are getting library cards because they\'re going to university, that could explain the increase. It suggests that the rise in library users isn\'t because of the libraries themselves but because students are using them for school. That could weaken the conclusion that the libraries caused the increase.\n\nC) Many citizens who got the first library card also got cards for the other two. So this is about overlap in card applications. If the same people applied for multiple libraries, it might mean that the increase in total cards isn\'t as big as it seems because of duplication. But wait, the context says by 2010, it was 130,000, and by 2012, it\'s over 200,000. So if the same people are applying multiple times, the actual number of unique users might not have tripled. But the conclusion is about the number of people coming in, not unique users. So maybe this doesn\'t directly affect the conclusion as much as B does.\n\nD) The new district is developing rapidly, and many migrants came in over four years. So if a lot of new people moved into the area where the new library is, that could explain the increase. The new library serves a growing population, so the number of people coming in isn\'t just because of the library but because the population is increasing. That could also weaken the conclusion.\n\nNow, comparing B and D. B suggests that students are using the libraries, which could be a separate reason for the i