the university markets its programs more aggressively, they won't need to reduce spending next year.\n\nWait, but the president's argument is that if they market more, they can increase enrollment, which would prevent spending cuts. So the assumption is that marketing more leads to enroll, which in turn leads to not reducing spending.\n\nSo the assumption is that marketing more leads to enroll, which is what option C is saying. Because option C says if they market more, they won't need to reduce spending. But actually, the president's argument is that not increasing enrollment leads to spending cuts, so to avoid spending cuts, they must increase enrollment, which requires marketing.\n\nWait, maybe I'm getting confused. Let me rephrase.\n\nPresident's argument:\n\nIf not enroll → reduce spending.\n\nTo maintain quality, must market more → which leads to enroll.\n\nSo the conclusion is that they must market more to maintain quality, which leads to enroll, which avoids spending cuts.\n\nSo the assumption is that marketing more leads to enroll, which is what the president is saying. So the assumption is that marketing more leads to enroll, which is option C.\n\nWait, but option C says: If the university markets its programs more aggressively, they won't need to reduce spending next year.\n\nBut the president's argument is that if they market more, they can increase enrollment, which would prevent spending cuts. So the assumption is that marketing more leads to enroll, which in turn leads to not reducing spending.\n\nSo the assumption is that marketing more leads to enroll, which is what option C is stating. So the correct answer should be C.\n\nWait, but let me check the other options again.\n\nOption B: If the university increases enrollment, they won't need to reduce spending. That seems like the contrapositive of the president's first statement. The president says not enroll → reduce spending. So the contrapositive is not reduce spending → enroll. So B is saying if enroll, then not reduce spending, which is the contrapositive. So B is actually a valid statement based on the president's argument.\n\nBut the question is about the conclusion being properly drawn if an assumption is made. The president's conclusion is that they must market more to maintain quality. So the assumption needed is that marketing more leads to enroll, which is option C.\n\nWait, but I'm getting confused because both B and C seem related. Let me think again.\n\nPresident's argument:\n\n1. If not enroll → reduce spending. (¬Enroll → Reduce)\n\n2. To maintain quality, must market more → which leads to enroll. (Maintain Quality → Market More → Enroll)\n\nSo the conclusion is that they must market more to maintain quality.\n\nBut the argument also connects not enroll to reduce spending, so the chain is:\n\n¬Enroll → Reduce\n\nBut to avoid Reduce, they must Enroll, which requires Marketing.\n\nSo the assumption is that Marketing leads to Enroll, which is option C.\n\nOption B is about if they Enroll, then they won't Reduce. Which is the contrapositive of the first statement, so it's a valid inference, but it's not the assumption needed for the conclusion.\n\nThe conclusion is about Marketing leading to Enroll, so the assumption is that Marketing leads to Enroll, which is option C.\n\nSo the answer should be C.\n"]
