ife. So, if someone follows self-discipline, they can maintain their rhythm and live a healthy life. So D seems to be a correct statement.\n\nWait, but the question is which can be obtained based on the statement. So both C and D seem to be correct. But let me check the options again.\n\nLooking back, the context says that self-discipline leads to not indulging, maintaining rhythm, and a healthy life. So C is a direct restatement of the context, and D is also a conclusion.\n\nBut the options are A, B, C, D, and I have to choose one. Let me see if both C and D are correct or if I'm missing something.\n\nOption C is a direct consequence: without self-discipline, you indulge. That's exactly what the context says.\n\nOption D is also correct because maintaining the rhythm (through self-discipline) leads to a healthy and happy life.\n\nWait, but the question is asking which can be obtained. So both C and D are correct, but looking at the options, maybe only one is correct. Let me check the options again.\n\nWait, the options are:\n\nA) All sports people are disciplined \u2013 not necessarily.\n\nB) Some lack self-discipline and don't lack health knowledge \u2013 the context doesn't say they don't lack health knowledge, just that they don't act on it.\n\nC) If you don't adhere to self-discipline, you'll indulge \u2013 yes, that's directly from the context.\n\nD) People who maintain their rhythm can live healthy and happy \u2013 yes, that's also from the context.\n\nBut the question is which can be obtained. So both C and D are correct, but looking at the options, maybe only one is intended.\n\nWait, the context says that self-discipline leads to not indulging, maintaining rhythm, and healthy life. So both C and D are correct. But in the options, only one can be chosen. Let me check the options again.\n\nWait, the options are A, B, C, D. So perhaps both C and D are correct, but the question might be expecting the most direct answer.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I'm overcomplicating. Let me think again.\n\nThe context says that people without self-discipline can't help but do things they know are bad. So C is a direct statement of that.\n\nD is also a conclusion, but it's more about the outcome of adhering to self-discipline.\n\nSo both C and D are correct, but perhaps the question is expecting the most direct answer, which is C.\n\nWait, but the options are separate, so maybe both are correct, but in the given options, only one is correct. Let me check the original question again.\n\nWait, the question is asking which can be obtained based on the statement. So both C and D are correct, but perhaps the answer is C because it's a direct consequence, while D is a broader outcome.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the answer is D because it's about the result of adhering to self-discipline, which is mentioned in the context.\n\nWait, the context says that adhering to self-discipline will lead to maintaining rhythm and living a healthy life. So D is correct.\n\nBut then C is also correct because not adhering leads to indulging.\n\nHmm, this is confusing. Let me think again.\n\nThe context says that people without self-discipline can't help but do things they know are bad. So C is a correct statement.\n\nD is also correct because the context says that adhering to self-discipline leads to a healthy life.\n\nSo both C and D are correct, but the options are separate. Wait, but the options are A, B, C, D, so perhaps both C and D are correct, but the question might be expecting the most direct answer.\n\nWait, but in the options, only one can be chosen. So perhaps the answer is C because it's a direct consequence, while D is a result.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the answer is D because it's about the outcome of adhering to self-discipline, which is mentioned.\n\nWait, I'm getting stuck. Let me try to approach it differently.\n\nThe context is about self-discipline leading to not indulging, maintaining rhythm, and living a healthy life.\n\nSo, the statement in the