g strategy.\n\nBetween A and C, both seem relevant. However, C directly addresses the legality and thus the validity of the strategy, making the argument against it (that it's not profitable) incorrect. A shows that the strategy might continue to work, which weakens the argument that it's not profitable.\n\nBut wait, the argument is that the method can't be profitable in the long run. If C is true, then the method is allowed, so the argument is weakened because the airline can legally do it and it might be profitable. A also weakens it by suggesting the airline might continue doing it, which could make it more profitable.\n\nI think C is a stronger answer because it directly undermines the argument by showing the strategy is allowed, whereas A is more about the airline's tendency to repeat the strategy, which might not necessarily make it profitable but shows it's a viable tactic.\n\nWait, but the argument is about the airline's method not being profitable. If C is true, then the method is allowed, so the airline can do it, which might make it profitable in the long run. So C weakens the argument by showing the method is allowed, thus making the argument that it's not profitable incorrect.\n\nYes, I think C is the best answer.\n"] 40%|████      | 242/600 [2:47:55<2:44:07, 27.51s/it]
