  "choices": [
    {
      "index": 0,
      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out which option best supports the experts' opinion that the decline of Easter Island's civilization isn't solely due to the felling of trees. Let me break this down step by step.\n\nFirst, the context says that Easter Island's civilization declined because people started logging palm forests, leading to loss of fertile soil and food shortages. But some experts think this isn't the whole story. So, the question is asking which of the options, if true, would support that experts' view.\n\nLooking at the options:\n\nOption A: Talks about cutting down palm trees around 1200 AD to build wooden boats and transport stone statues. This supports the idea that tree cutting was happening, which aligns with the original cause, so it doesn't support the experts' opinion that tree cutting isn't the main factor. So probably not A.\n\nOption B: Archaeological findings show that after the last tree was felled, there were still people living and farming didn't decline. This directly contradicts the original claim because if the last tree was felled and people continued without issues, it shows that the decline wasn't because of the trees. So B supports the experts.\n\nOption C: Pollen analysis shows forests were destroyed starting in 800 AD, with less palm pollen over time. This supports the original argument that trees were being cut down, so it doesn't help the experts. So probably not C.\n\nOption D: Dutch colonists came in 1772 and enslaved locals, reducing population. This is more about population decline due to external factors, not about tree cutting causing soil loss. So D doesn't directly address the experts' point about tree cutting not being the main cause. So probably not D.\n\nSo the best option is B because it shows that even after trees were felled, the people continued to farm, supporting the idea that tree cutting wasn't the main cause of decline.\n</think>\n\nThe correct answer is B. \n\n**Answer:** B) Archaeological findings show that after the last tree (palm tree) on the island was felled, there were still a large number of indigenous people living, and the level of agricultural farming did not decline.\n\n**Explanation:** Option B supports the experts' view by demonstrating that the decline wasn't solely due to tree cutting, as people continued to farm successfully after trees were felled.",
