f they eat dinner at home less often, then the total time spent preparing dinner would be less because they're eating out more. But the context says they spend less time preparing dinner, not that they eat out more. So if they eat out more, they might not prepare as much, but the quality of the dinners they do prepare might still be similar. So this could explain why the time spent is less but the quality isn't affected because they're eating out more often. That might help resolve the discrepancy.\n\nOption C: Adults who do not work outside the home tend to prepare breakfast more often than adults who work outside the home.\n\nThis is about breakfast preparation frequency. The context is about dinner preparation time and quality. So this might not directly relate to the issue. It's possible that they prepare breakfast more often, but that doesn't explain why dinner at home is similar in quality despite spending less time preparing it.\n\nOption D: Adults who work outside the home spend 2 hours less per day on all household responsibilities, including dinner preparation, than do adults who do not work outside the home.\n\nWait, so if they spend 2 hours less per day on all household responsibilities, including dinner prep, that's a significant amount of time saved. But the context says they spend 100 minutes less per week on dinner preparation. Let me convert that to daily time. 100 minutes is about 1.67 hours per week. Divided by 7 days, that's roughly 0.24 hours per day, which is about 14.5 minutes less per day. But option D says they spend 2 hours less per day on all household responsibilities, including dinner prep. That's a much bigger difference than what's mentioned in the context. So this seems inconsistent. Maybe I'm misunderstanding.\n\nWait, the context says 100 minutes less per week, which is about 1.67 hours per week. So per day, that's about 0.24 hours, which is about 14.5 minutes less per day. But option D says 2 hours less per day on all household responsibilities, including dinner prep. That's a much larger difference. So if they're spending 2 hours less per day on all household tasks, including dinner prep, that would mean they're saving a lot more time than the 14.5 minutes per day mentioned in the context. So this seems inconsistent. Maybe I'm miscalculating.\n\nWait, perhaps the 100 minutes less per week is the total time spent on dinner preparation, but the total household responsibilities are more than just dinner prep. So if they're saving 2 hours per day on all household responsibilities, that would mean they're saving a lot more time than just dinner prep. So maybe the time saved on dinner prep is part of a larger time saving on other tasks. So this could explain why they spend less time on dinner prep but the quality remains the same because they're not cutting down on other things. But I'm not sure if this directly addresses the discrepancy.\n\nNow, going back to option B. If adults who work outside the home eat dinner at home less often, then the total time they spend preparing dinner would be less because they're eating out more. So even though they spend less time preparing each dinner, they're eating out more often, so the dinners they do prepare might still be similar in quality, variety, and nutritional value. That would explain why the quality isn't affected despite spending less time preparing.\n\nOption D seems to suggest that they're spending a lot less time on all household responsibilities, including dinner prep. But the context only mentions a 100-minute difference in dinner prep time. So if they're saving a lot more time on other tasks, that could mean they have more free time for other things, but it's not directly connected to the dinner prep time. So maybe this isn't the best explanation.\n\nOption B seems more plausible because it directly relates to the frequency of eating at home, which ties into the time spent preparing dinner. If they eat at home less often, they might not be preparing as much, but the dinners they do prepare could still be similar in quality because they're not cutting down on other aspects.\n\nSo, I think the answer is B.\n</think>\n\nThe correct answer is B.\n\nStep-by-Step Explanation:\n\n1. **Understanding the Discrepancy**: The context states that working adults spend less time preparing dinner at home but their home-dinners are similar in nutritional value, variety, and course count. This seems contradictory.\n\n2. **Analyzing Option B**: If working adults eat dinner at home less often, they might spend less time preparing it, but the quality remains the same because they're eating out more, not necessarily cutting back on dinner prep quality.\n\n3. **Other Options**: Options A, C, and D don't directly address the time spent on dinner prep or explain why quality remains consistent despite less prep time.\n\n4. **Conclusion**: Option B best explains the discrepancy by linking less frequent home dinners to reduced prep time without affecting quality.\n\nAnswer: B",
