d and taking time, but it doesn\'t specifically mention small cities being better. It\'s more about the city\'s state rather than its size. So B might be a possibility, but I\'m not sure if it\'s the best fit.\n\nOption C: "In cities with a long development time, the popularity of his gathering is relatively strong, so that the city is mature." Wait, the context says the opposite. It says that cities take several years to mature, and popularity doesn\'t gather in a day. So if the context is that popularity doesn\'t come quickly, then C seems to contradict that. It suggests that longer development time leads to more popularity, which isn\'t what the context is saying. So C doesn\'t support the viewpoint.\n\nOption D: "Large cities have large populations and are well matured." This aligns with the idea that larger cities are more developed because they have more people. The context mentions that Shenzhen, except Luohu, is unfinished, implying that it\'s not yet large or mature. So D supports the idea that larger cities are more mature, which is consistent with the viewpoint that cities take time to develop and are not just about size.\n\nWait, but the context says that Shenzhen is unfinished, so maybe it\'s not a large city yet. So D might be supporting the idea that larger cities are more mature, which is in line with the point that cities take time to develop.\n\nSo, putting it together, the best option that supports the idea that cities are unfinished and take time to develop, with larger ones being more mature, is D.\n'] 48%|████▊     | 288/600 [3:13:00<6:23:23, 73.73s/it] 
