. So, the context is pointing out that effort and knowledge don't necessarily translate to high ability or creativity.\n\nNow, looking at the options:\n\nA) There are many knowledgeable scholars who have no creative ability.\nHmm, the context says some knowledgeable scholars lack creative ability, but does it say many? Maybe, but I'm not sure if it's the main point.\n\nB) Only with creative ability can profound knowledge be realized.\nThis seems opposite. The context is saying that even with knowledge and effort, you don't necessarily have creativity. So this might not be supported.\n\nC) People who are creative may not have profound knowledge.\nThe context is about knowledgeable scholars who lack creativity. It doesn't directly say that creative people don't have knowledge, but it's related. Maybe.\n\nD) People with profound knowledge may not have the ability to create.\nThis seems similar to C. The context supports that knowledgeable people might not have creative ability, so this could be the answer.\n\nWait, let me think again. The context is about knowledgeable scholars who, despite their effort, lack ability, opinions, and creativity. So it's saying that having knowledge and effort doesn't guarantee high ability or creativity. So the argument supported is that people with profound knowledge may not have the ability to create. That's option D.\n\nAlternatively, option C says people who are creative may not have profound knowledge. But the context doesn't directly address that. It's about knowledgeable people lacking creativity, not about creative people lacking knowledge.\n\nSo I think D is the correct answer because it directly reflects the context's point that knowledgeable people may not be able to create.\n"]
