blem.\n\nBut the question is about whether the plan will not achieve its objective. The objective is to reduce deaths from viruses. So if the virus is still present, the deaths would still happen, meaning the plan fails.\n\nBut the question is about which option would best indicate that the plan won't work. So both C and D are possible, but I think C is more about the physical elimination of the bat, while D is about the virus still being present.\n\nWait, but the government's plan is to eliminate the bat. If the virus is still present because the bat is gone, then the deaths would still happen. So D shows that the virus is still around, so the plan fails.\n\nBut C is about the bat's location. If the government can't fully cut off all passageways, the bat can still come out, so the virus re-emerges. So both C and D are correct, but I think the answer is C because it's about the feasibility of the plan. If the government can't fully eliminate the bat, the plan fails.\n\nWait, but the question is about whether the plan will not lead to its objective. The objective is to reduce deaths. So if the virus is still present, the deaths would still happen. So D shows that the virus is still present, so the plan fails.\n\nBut I'm a bit confused. Let me think again.\n\nThe government's plan is to eliminate the spotted bat by cutting off its passageways. If the virus is only transmitted by the spotted bat, then eliminating it should stop the virus. But if the virus is still present because the bat is gone, then the deaths would still happen.\n\nSo D shows that the virus is still present because the serum worked, meaning the virus isn't eliminated. So the plan fails.\n\nC shows that the government can't fully eliminate the bat, so the virus re-emerges. So both C and D are correct, but I think the answer is C because it's about the physical elimination, while D is about the virus still being present.\n\nWait, but the question is about whether the plan will not lead to its objective. The objective is to reduce deaths. So if the virus is still present, the deaths would still happen. So D is more directly showing that the virus is still around, making the plan fail.\n\nBut I'm not sure. Maybe the answer is C because it's about the feasibility of the plan. If the government can't cut off all passageways, the plan fails.\n\nI think I need to choose between C and D. Let me read the options again.\n\nOption C: The spotted bat is deep inside the caves, and government workers can't penetrate completely. So the government's plan is to cut off passageways, but if they can't fully do so, the bat can still come out, so the virus re-emerges. This would mean the plan fails because the virus is still present.\n\nOption D: Villagers who would have died from a virus were cured by a serum from a local flower whose only means of germinating is via the fur of the spotted fruit bat. So the virus is still present because the serum worked. This also means the plan fails.\n\nSo both C and D are correct, but I think the answer is C because it's about the physical elimination, while D is about the virus still being present.\n\nWait, but the question is about whether the plan will not lead to its objective. The objective is to reduce deaths. So if the virus is still present, the deaths would still happen. So D is more directly showing that the virus is still around, making the plan fail.\n\nBut I'm not sure. Maybe the answer is C because it's about the feasibility of the plan. If the government can't cut off all passageways, the plan fails.\n\nI think I'll go with C because it's about the physical elimination, making it more likely that the plan won't work.\n"]
