  "choices": [
    {
      "index": 0,
      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to figure out which option best describes Gamba's argumentation strategy based on the given context. Let me start by reading the context carefully.\n\nGamba is responding to Munoz's claim that the Southwest Hopeville Neighbors Association overwhelmingly opposes the new water system. Munoz bases this on the association passing a resolution opposing the system. However, Gamba points out that only 25 out of 350 members voted, with 10 in favor and 15 against. Gamba argues that this small sample size doesn't represent the majority's view because 15 opposing votes are less than 1% of the population. Therefore, Gamba is cautioning against assuming that a few votes indicate the overall public opinion.\n\nNow, looking at the options:\n\nA) Criticizing a view on the grounds that the view is based on evidence that is in principle impossible to disconfirm. This seems to be about the strength of evidence. Gamba isn't questioning the evidence itself but rather the conclusion drawn from it. So maybe not A.\n\nB) Attempting to cast doubt on a conclusion by claiming the sample is too small. This fits because Gamba is pointing out that the sample size is too small to reliably represent the population. So B looks like a strong candidate.\n\nC) Refuting an argument by showing premises don't guarantee the conclusion. Gamba isn't directly attacking the premises but rather the conclusion drawn from them. So C might not be the best fit.\n\nD) Questioning a conclusion based on a vote's results, noting that certain views are more likely to vote. Gamba is indeed questioning the conclusion based on the vote, but he's not pointing out that people with certain views are more likely to vote. Instead, he's pointing out the sample size issue. So D might not be the right choice.\n\nSo, putting it together, Gamba is using option B to cast doubt on the conclusion by highlighting the small sample size.\n</think>\n\nThe correct answer is B) Attempting to cast doubt on a conclusion by claiming that the statistical sample on which the conclusion is based is too small to be dependable.\n\n**Answer: B**",
