air costs will drop more than collision numbers.\n\nOption A suggests that the least experienced crew is causing most collisions. If that's true, then training them would reduce collisions, which makes sense. But does it directly explain why repair costs would drop more?\n\nOption B says collisions happen without passengers. I'm not sure how that ties into repair costs. Maybe it affects how often repairs are needed, but it doesn't directly link to the cost reduction.\n\nOption C says the training will focus on the most costly collisions. If they avoid those, then the overall repair costs would drop more because those high-cost collisions are being reduced. That seems like a logical reason for the bigger cost reduction.\n\nOption D talks about the cost including wages during training. That might affect the budget, but it doesn't directly explain why repair costs would drop more. It's more about the cost structure rather than the outcome.\n\nSo, putting it together, the best answer is probably C because it directly addresses reducing the most expensive repairs, leading to a larger drop in total repair costs.\n"]
