      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out which option supports the market analysts' prediction that old-style bulky detergents will disappear because the three major manufacturers are switching to concentrated detergents in smaller packages. Let me break this down step by step.\n\nFirst, the context says that these three manufacturers are reducing inactive ingredients, making their detergents more concentrated. They're selling smaller packages, which probably means more of the active ingredient per package. They're citing cost savings from reducing packaging and production costs. The analysts predict that because these three control 80% of the market, their move will lead to the old detergents disappearing.\n\nNow, the question is asking which option, if true, would most strongly support that prediction. So I need to find an option that shows the new concentrated detergents are becoming more popular or replacing the old ones.\n\nLooking at the options:\n\nA) Smaller manufacturers can't retool factories. Hmm, this is about the smaller manufacturers' costs. But the question is about the market analysts' prediction, which is about the big three's move. So maybe this doesn't directly support the prediction because it's about the smaller ones, not the bigger ones.\n\nB) Consumers will pay more per load. If the new detergents cost more per load, that might make people hesitant to switch, which would contradict the prediction. So this doesn't support the idea that old detergents disappear.\n\nC) Consumers are persuaded by environmental concerns to buy concentrated detergents. This seems relevant because the concentrated detergents are more aligned with environmental concerns (like reducing cardboard waste). If consumers are being influenced by these concerns, they might prefer the new detergents, leading to their increase in sales and possibly driving the old ones out of the market. This supports the analysts' prediction.\n\nD) Supermarkets not charging less for display. This might affect how much manufacturers charge, but it doesn't directly address why consumers would switch to the new detergents. It's more about pricing strategy than consumer preference.\n\nSo, putting it together, option C directly addresses consumer behavior influenced by environmental concerns, which is a key factor in the analysts' prediction. The other options either don't affect consumer choice or are about the smaller manufacturers, which aren't the focus here.\n</think>\n\nThe correct answer is **C**.\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe market analysts predict that the move by the three major manufacturers to concentrated detergents will lead to the disappearance of old-style bulky detergents. This prediction hinges on increased consumer preference for the new detergents. \n\nOption C directly supports this prediction by stating that consumers are increasingly persuaded by environmental concerns to buy the concentrated detergents. This aligns with the analysts' expectation that consumer behavior will shift in favor of the new product, driving its adoption and potentially displacing the old detergents. \n\nOther options either address unrelated factors (like costs for smaller manufacturers) or do not influence consumer choice as effectively. Therefore, option C is the strongest support for the prediction.",
