 they interpret it the same way might not be true. So, this could be the exception.\n\nD) A reader's interpretation evolves over time.\n- Since readers' systems of beliefs change over time, their interpretations of the same poem could evolve. This seems plausible.\n\nSo, the question is which one could NOT be true. Based on this, option C seems to be the one that cannot be true because the critic's point suggests that different readers from different eras have different systems, leading to different interpretations. Therefore, a modern reader and a nineteenth-century reader are unlikely to interpret a Shakespearean sonnet the same way.\n</think>\n\nThe correct answer is C.\n\n**Answer: C) A modern reader and a nineteenth-century reader interpret one of Shakespeare's sonnets in the same way.**\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe critic's statements imply that a poem's meaning is shaped by the reader's unique system of beliefs. Since different readers from various eras have distinct belief systems, their interpretations of the same poem are likely to differ. Therefore, it is unlikely that a modern reader and a nineteenth-century reader would interpret a Shakespearean sonnet identically.",
