eader's. However, knowing how the poet viewed their work might provide deeper understanding, enhancing enjoyment. This could be true.\n\nC) A modern reader and a nineteenth-century reader interpret a Shakespearean sonnet the same way.\n- The critic's point is about different belief systems leading to different interpretations. Shakespeare's sonnets were written in a specific time, and a modern reader's beliefs are different from a nineteenth-century one. So, they might interpret the sonnet differently. Therefore, this statement saying they interpret it the same way might not be true. So, this could be the exception.\n\nD) A reader's interpretation evolves over time.\n- Since readers' systems of beliefs change over time, their interpretations of the same poem could evolve. This seems plausible.\n\nSo, the question is which one could NOT be true. Based on this, option C seems to be the one that cannot be true because the critic's point suggests that different readers from different eras have different systems, leading to different interpretations. Therefore, a modern reader and a nineteenth-century reader are unlikely to interpret a Shakespearean sonnet the same way.\n"]
