tion says.\n\n   Option A: There is a pen in the second drawer. Well, from above, if C is false, that's true. So Option A would be true. But wait, we're assuming only A is true, so if A is true, then Option A is true, which would mean two truths: A and the option. But the question says only one is true. So this can't be the case because if A is true, and Option A is also true, that's two truths, which violates the condition. So this scenario is invalid.\n\n2. **Assume B is the only one telling the truth:**\n   - B says all drawers have books. So, A is false, meaning every drawer has books.\n   - C says no pen in the second drawer. If B is true, C could be true or false, but since only B is true, C must be false. So, there is a pen in the second drawer.\n   - D says letters in the third drawer. If B is true, D could be true or false, but since only B is true, D must be false. So, no letters in the third drawer.\n   \n   Now, what does this mean? All drawers have books (from B). Pen is in the second drawer (from C being false). No letters in the third drawer (from D being false).\n   \n   So, the drawers would have books in all, pen in second, and no letters in third. But the options are about specific drawers. Let's check the options:\n\n   Option B: There are letters in the third drawer. But D is false, so no letters. So Option B is false. Option C: There is no pen in the second drawer. But C is false, so there is a pen. So Option C is true. But we're assuming only B is true, so if Option C is also true, that's two truths, which is invalid. So this scenario is also invalid.\n\n3. **Assume C is the only one telling the truth:**\n   - C says no pen in the second drawer. So, pen is not in the second drawer.\n   - A says no books in some drawers. If C is true, A could be true or false, but since only C is true, A must be false. So, every drawer has books.\n   - B says all drawers have books. If A is false, B is true. But we're assuming only C is true, so B must be false. Wait, that's a contradiction because if A is false, B must be true. So this scenario is impossible because A and B are related. If A is false, B is true, which can't happen if only C is true. So this scenario is invalid.\n\n4. **Assume D is the only one telling the truth:**\n   - D says there are letters in the third drawer. So, the third drawer has letters.\n   - A says no books in some drawers. If D is true, A could be true or false, but since only D is true, A must be false. So, every drawer has books.\n   - B says all drawers have books. If A is false, B is true. But we're assuming only D is true, so B must be false. Contradiction again because if A is false, B is true. So this scenario is invalid.\n\nWait, that can't be right. Let me re-examine. If D is true, then letters are in the third drawer. A says no books in some drawers, which would make A false, meaning all drawers have books. So B would be true because all drawers have books. But if only D is true, then B must be false, which contradicts because if A is false, B is true. So this scenario is impossible.\n\nHmm, so none of the scenarios where only one person is telling the truth seem to work except maybe the first one, but it led to a contradiction with the options. Maybe I made a mistake in the first scenario.\n\nWait, in the first scenario, when A is true, we concluded that Option A is also true, which can't be. So perhaps the only valid scenario is when only C is true, but that led to a contradiction because A and B would both be true. Alternatively, maybe I need to consider that if only C is true, then A is false, meaning all drawers have books, which would make B true, which can't happen. So perhaps the only valid scenario is when only D is true, but that also leads to a contradiction because A being false would make B true.\n\nWait, maybe I'm missing something. Let's try another approach. Let's consider each option and see if it must be true if only one of A, B, C, D is true.\n\nOption A: There 