 think this is supported. The context doesn\'t say anything about alternative defenses, so B might not be correct.\n\nOption C: "It is not possible for a person born with albinism to adopt other artificial protective measures against excessive exposure to the sun."\n\nThis seems a bit off. The context doesn\'t say anything about whether people with albinism can or cannot use protective measures like sunscreen or hats. It just says they are more susceptible because they can\'t make melanin. So C is making a statement that\'s not supported by the context. It\'s introducing a new idea about artificial measures that the context doesn\'t address.\n\nOption D: "When a non-albino person gets sunburn, the amount of melanin produced by that person decreases."\n\nWait, the context says that albinism inhibits melanin production. It doesn\'t say anything about what happens when someone gets sunburned. In fact, getting sunburned usually causes the skin to produce more melanin to protect itself, right? So this seems contradictory. The context doesn\'t support D because it\'s talking about a decrease in melanin when someone gets sunburned, which is the opposite of what usually happens.\n\nSo, putting it all together, the only option that makes sense is A. The context directly supports the idea that melanin protects against sunburn and related issues because people with albinism can\'t make it and thus are more susceptible. The other options either introduce new information not supported by the context or present the opposite of what\'s stated.\n']
