final_output:  ["\n\nThe expert's view is supported by the fact that traditional bank branches are losing their main service channels due to the rise of online and mobile banking. \n\n**Answer: D**", "\nOkay, so I need to figure out which option best supports the expert's view that traditional bank branches will disappear within 20 years. Let me start by understanding the context provided.\n\nThe context says that community banks in big cities are closing often, but new types of banks like unmanned banks, 5G banks, and smart banks are popping up. The traditional functions of bank branches, like cold glass counters and metal benches, are being replaced. Instead, they're offering more customized services, product experiences, social interactions, etc. Experts predict that traditional branches will disappear in 20 years.\n\nSo the expert is arguing that because of these changes, traditional branches won't last. Now, the question is asking which option, if true, would best support this view.\n\nLooking at the options:\n\nA) Customers have to enter to take a number, wait, and deal with simple business, taking a long time. This seems to talk about the process of getting a number and waiting, which might relate to the decline of traditional services, but it's more about the process rather than the disappearance of the branch itself.\n\nB) AI and other tech change people's impression of branches. This could mean that branches become less necessary or that people see them as outdated, supporting the idea that they might disappear.\n\nC) Complex business needs face-to-face at branches, like account opening or cancellation. This suggests that some services are still necessary at branches, which might argue against the expert's view, but it's not directly about the disappearance.\n\nD) Online and mobile banking are the main channels, weakening traditional branches. This directly supports the idea that traditional branches are declining because their main service channels are becoming less used.\n\nSo, the expert is saying that because of technological changes and the shift to online/mobile banking, traditional branches will disappear. Option D mentions that these channels are weakening, which would support the expert's prediction. Option B also relates to technology changing people's perception, but D is more about the decline in usage of the branches themselves.\n\nTherefore, the best answer is D.\n"]
