time to read as much.\n\nI think both A and C are possible, but let's look at the options again.\n\nOption A: Children have schoolwork, so they don't have time to read. That would explain why their per capita reading is down, even though parents are reading more.\n\nOption C: Children are listening to books instead of reading, so their per capita reading (as measured by actual reading) is down.\n\nBut the context doesn't specify whether the increase in books read includes both parents and children. It just says the amount increased. So, if parents are reading more, but children are reading less, the total could still go up.\n\nWait, the context says the amount of books read by parents and children increased by 1.8 percentage points. So, if parents read more, and children read a bit less, but the total is still up because parents' increase is more than the decrease in children's reading.\n\nBut the per capita for children went down, meaning that the number of books read by children didn't increase enough to offset the decrease in their own reading.\n\nSo, if children are listening to books instead of reading them, their per capita reading would decrease, even if parents are reading more.\n\nAlternatively, if schoolwork is taking up time, children might not have time to read as much.\n\nI think both A and C are possible, but let's see which one is more likely.\n\nOption A is about schoolwork burden and extracurricular reading. The context mentions that in 2018, children read 4.72 books per person, down 0.6 percentage points from 2017. So, the decrease is small, but it's still a decrease.\n\nOption C is about children listening to books, which isn't counted. So, if parents are reading more, but children are just listening, their per capita reading would decrease.\n\nBut the context doesn't specify whether the increase in books read includes both parents and children. It just says the amount increased. So, if parents are reading more, but children are reading less, the total could still go up.\n\nWait, the context says the amount of books read by parents and children increased by 1.8 percentage points. So, if parents read more, and children read a bit less, but the total is still up because parents' increase is more than the decrease in children's reading.\n\nBut the per capita for children went down, meaning that the number of books read by children didn't increase enough to offset the decrease in their own reading.\n\nSo, if children are listening to books instead of reading them, their per capita reading would decrease, even if parents are reading more.\n\nAlternatively, if schoolwork is taking up time, children might not have time to read as much.\n\nI think both A and C are possible, but let's look at the options again.\n\nOption A: Children have schoolwork and don't have time to read. That makes sense because schoolwork is a significant part of their time.\n\nOption C: Children are listening to books instead of reading. That could also explain it, but it's more about the method of reading rather than the time spent.\n\nThe context doesn't mention anything about extracurricular reading or schoolwork, but it does mention that the city's children read fewer books per person. So, the most straightforward explanation is that schoolwork is taking up their time, making them read less.\n\nTherefore, I think option A is the best explanation.\n</think>\n\nThe phenomenon described is that while the total number of books read by parents and children increased, the per capita reading for children decreased. This suggests that even though parents are reading more, children are not reading as much on average. \n\nOption A explains this by pointing out that children have a heavy schoolwork burden, which limits their time for reading. This aligns with the context, as schoolwork could be a significant factor reducing children's reading time despite parents increasing their reading.\n\nOption C suggests that children are listening to books instead of reading them, which isn't counted. While plausible, the context doesn't specify that listening to books is a factor, making this explanation less direct.\n\nOption B is about parents' education, which doesn't relate to the per capita decrease. Option D talks about digital reading, which isn't mentioned in the context.\n\nThus, the best explanation is Option A, as it directly addresses the time constraint from schoolwork affecting children's reading.\n\nAnswer: A",
