uld mean the argument is weakened because she would object.\n\nAlternatively, if the executor must sell it because it's the only way to clear the debts, then the executor has no choice, so Loux wouldn't object because she didn't care. So, B might not weaken the argument.\n\nOption C says Loux was aware of the legal requirements. If she was aware, maybe she knew that the executor had to satisfy certain conditions, and selling the farm might not meet those. So, even if she didn't care about the farm, she might object because she knows the executor needs to do more than just sell it. That would weaken the argument because her awareness could mean she would object.\n\nSo, between C and D. The question is which one most weakens the argument.\n\nIf C is true, Loux knew the executor had to satisfy certain conditions, so she might object even if she didn't care about the farm. That would weaken the argument because her awareness could mean she would object.\n\nIf D is true, the grandson wants the farm, so Loux might prefer transferring it, thus having an objection. That also weakens the argument.\n\nBut the question is which one is more likely to weaken the argument. The solicitor's argument is that Loux wouldn't object because she didn't care. If Loux was aware of the legal requirements, she might have known that selling the farm wasn't enough, so she might object. That directly addresses the reason why she wouldn't object, making C a strong contender.\n\nAlternatively, if the grandson wants it, maybe Loux would prefer transferring it, but the context says she didn't express any particular desire, so maybe she doesn't care, but if the grandson wants it, she might prefer that. So, D could also be a reason.\n\nBut I think C is more about the executor's obligations, which would make Loux object regardless of the grandson's desire. So, C is more about the legal requirements, which would make her object, thus weakening the argument.\n\nTherefore, the answer is C.\n</think>\n\nThe correct answer is C.\n\nStep-by-step explanation:\n\nThe solicitor's argument is that Loux wouldn't object to selling the Stoke Farm because she didn't express any particular desire about it. However, if Loux was aware of the legal requirements the executor must satisfy, she might have known that selling the farm wasn't sufficient to clear the estate's debts. This awareness could mean she would object to selling the farm, thus weakening her argument that she wouldn't object. \n\nAnswer: C",
