n nucleic acids but in nutrition.\n2. Advertisements claiming that aging or sickness weakens gene production are scientifically unfounded.\n\nNow, looking at the options:\n\nA) All advertisements about health products lack scientific basis.\nHmm, the context says that some ads are scientifically unfounded, but it doesn't say all. So I can't infer that all do. Maybe some are, but not all. So A might not be correct.\n\nB) Nucleic acid in the human body will decrease with age.\nThe context doesn't mention anything about nucleic acid levels decreasing. It talks about gene production being weak, but not necessarily nucleic acid levels. So B might not be directly inferred.\n\nC) Illness is the result of malnutrition.\nThe context says that the body lacks nutrition, not nucleic acids. So it's arguing against the idea that malnutrition is the cause of illness. Therefore, C is probably not correct because it's the opposite of what's stated.\n\nD) Eating health products may not promote your health.\nThe context mentions that nucleic acids in health products aren't helpful because the body doesn't need them as supplements. So eating such products might not be beneficial. This seems to align with D.\n\nWait, but let me double-check. The context doesn't directly say that health products aren't good, but it does say that the idea of needing exogenous nucleic acids is not supported. So it's more about the content of the products rather than the products themselves. However, the argument against the ads implies that promoting gene production isn't effective, which could relate to health products. So D seems the best fit.\n"] 62%|██████▏   | 369/600 [4:07:38<34:11,  8.88s/it] 62%|██████▏   | 370/600 [4:07:41<27:31,  7.18s/it]
