      "text": "<think>\nAlright, let's try to figure out this problem step by step. So, we have four applicants: Yan Zi, Zeng Yin, Meng Shen, and Xun Chen. They're applying for a project where the moderator can only be one of them, and the team can have up to two members, including the moderator. So, the team size can be one (just the moderator) or two (moderator plus one other).\n\nEach of them made a statement when applying. Let's list them again:\n\n1. Yan Zi says: If I become the host, I will invite Zeng Yin or Xun Chen as members.\n2. Zeng Yin says: If I become the host, I will invite Yan Zi or Meng Shen as members.\n3. Xun Chen says: I can be the host only if Yan Zi becomes a member of the project team.\n4. Meng Shen says: Only Xun Chen or Yan Zi can become a member of the project team; I can become the host.\n\nWe need to determine which combination of project team members is impossible, given that all four statements are true.\n\nFirst, let's understand what each statement implies.\n\nStarting with Yan Zi's statement: If she is the host, she will invite either Zeng Yin or Xun Chen. So, if Yan is host, the team would be Yan plus either Zeng or Xun. But she doesn't say anything about who she would invite if she's not the host. So, if she's not the host, she might not be part of the team, or she could be part of the team as a member, but the statement only specifies her actions if she's the host.\n\nZeng Yin's statement: If she's the host, she will invite either Yan or Meng. So similar to Yan, if she's host, she'll invite one of them, but doesn't specify if she's not the host.\n\nXun Chen's statement: He can be the host only if Yan is a member. So, if Xun is host, Yan must be a member. But this doesn't say anything about Xun being a member if he's not the host. So, if Xun is not the host, he might still be a member, but it's not required.\n\nMeng Shen's statement: Only Xun or Yan can be members; she can be the host. So, if Meng is the host, the only possible members are Xun or Yan. But if Meng is not the host, she might still be a member, but it's not required.\n\nWait, but the team can have up to two members, including the moderator. So, the team can be just the moderator, or the moderator plus one other.\n\nLet's consider each possible host and see what the team would be.\n\nCase 1: Yan Zi is the host.\n\nFrom Yan's statement, she will invite Zeng or Xun. So, the team would be Yan plus either Zeng or Xun. But we need to check if this is consistent with the other statements.\n\nIf Yan is host, then Zeng's statement is about if she's host, which she's not, so Zeng's statement doesn't affect this case.\n\nXun's statement: If Xun is host, Yan must be a member. But since Yan is host here, Xun's statement doesn't impose any restriction because Xun isn't the host. So, Xun could be a member or not, but since Yan is the host, Xun's statement doesn't apply.\n\nMeng's statement: If Meng is the host, then members can only be Xun or Yan. But since Yan is the host, Meng's statement doesn't restrict her being a member or not. However, if Meng is a member, she must be either Xun or Yan, but since she's not the host, she could be a member, but it's not required.\n\nWait, but if Yan is the host, then the team is Yan plus either Zeng or Xun. Let's see if that's possible.\n\nIf Yan is host and invites Zeng, then the team is Yan and Zeng. Let's check if this satisfies all statements.\n\nYan's statement: Yes, she invited Zeng.\n\nZeng's statement: If Zeng is host, she would invite Yan or Meng. But in this case, Zeng is a member, not the host, so Zeng's statement doesn't apply.\n\nXun's statement: If Xun is host, Yan must be a member. But Xun isn't the host here, so no issue.\n\nMeng's statement: If Meng is the host, members can only be Xun or Yan. But since Meng isn't the host, her statement doesn't impose any restrictions on her being a member or not. So, she could be a member, but it's not required.\n\nWait, but in this scenario, the team is Yan and Zeng. So, Meng isn't a member, which is fine because her statement only restricts who can be members if she's the host, not if she's a member.\n\nSo, this seems possible.\n\nCase 2: Zeng Yin is the host.\n\nFrom Zeng's statement, she will invite Yan or Meng. So, the team would be Zeng plus either Yan or Meng.\n\nLet's check if this is possible.\n\nIf Zeng is host and invites Yan, then the team is Zeng and Yan.\n\nCheck statements:\n\nYan's statement: If Yan is host, she invites Zeng or Xun. But here, Yan is a member, not the host, so Yan's statement doesn't apply.\n\nZeng's statement: Yes, she invited Yan.\n\nXun's statement: If Xun is host, Yan must be a member. But Xun isn't the host here, so no issue.\n\nMeng's statement: If Meng is the host, members can only be Xun or Yan. But here, Meng isn't the host, so her statement doesn't restrict her being a member or not. So, she could be a member, but it's not required.\n\nAlternatively, if Zeng invites Meng, then the team is Zeng and Meng.\n\nCheck:\n\nYan's statement: Yan isn't host, so no issue.\n\nZeng's statement: Yes, she invited Meng.\n\nXun's statement: Xun isn't host, so no issue.\n\nMeng's statement: If Meng is host, members can only be Xun or Yan. But here, Meng is host, so the members must be Xun or Yan. But in this case, the members are Zeng and Meng, which includes Meng as host and Zeng as member. But Meng's statement says that if she's host, the members can only be Xun or Yan. So, having Zeng as a member when Meng is host violates her statement. Therefore, this scenario is impossible.\n\nSo, if Zeng is host, she must invite Yan, making the team Zeng and Yan.\n\nCase 3: Xun Chen is the host.\n\nFrom Xun's statement, he can be host only if Yan is a member. So, if Xun is host, Yan must be a member.\n\nFrom Meng's statement, if Meng is host, members can only be Xun or Yan. But if Xun is host, Meng's statement doesn't restrict her being a member or not, but if Meng is a member, she must be Xun or Yan.\n\nWait, but if Xun is host, then the team would be Xun plus Yan (from Xun's statement). So, the team is Xun and Yan.\n\nCheck if this is possible.\n\nYan's statement: If Yan is host, she invites Zeng or Xun. But here, Yan is a member, not the host, so no issue.\n\nZeng's statement: If Zeng is host, she invites Yan or Meng. But Zeng isn't host here, so no issue.\n\nMeng's statement: If Meng is host, members can only be Xun or Yan. But here, Meng isn't host, so her statement doesn't apply. So, she could be a member, but it's not required.\n\nAlternatively, if Xun is host and Meng is a member, then Meng's statement says that if she's host, members can only be Xun or Yan. But here, Xun is host, so Meng can be a member, but she's not required to be. So, the team could be Xun, Yan, and Meng. But wait, the team can have up to two members, including the host. So, if Xun is host, the team can be Xun plus one other. So, if Xun is host and Meng is a member, that would make the team Xun, Meng, and Yan? Wait, no, because the team can only have up to two members, including the host. So, if Xun is host, the team can be Xun plus one other. So, if Xun is host and Meng is a member, the team would be Xun and Meng. But Xun's statement requires that if he's host, Yan must be a member. So, the team would have to be Xun, Meng, and Yan, but that's three people, which exceeds the maximum of two. Therefore, this is impossible.\n\nWait, that's a problem. So, if Xun is host, he must have Yan as a member. So, the team would be Xun and Yan. But then, can Meng be a member? If Meng is a member, she must be either Xun or Yan, but she's not, so she can't be a member. So, the team would be Xun and Yan only.\n\nBut wait, the team can have up to two members, including the host. So, if Xun is host, the team can be Xun plus one other. So, if Xun is host, the team is Xun and either Yan or Meng. But Xun's statement requires that if he's host, Yan must be a member. So, the team must be Xun and Yan. Therefore, Meng cannot be a member in this case.\n\nAlternatively, if Xun is host and Meng is not a member, then the team is Xun and Yan. That's acceptable.\n\nSo, the possible team when Xun is host is Xun and Yan.\n\nCase 4: Meng Shen is the host.\n\nFrom Meng's statement, only Xun or Yan can be members. So, the team would be Meng plus either Xun or Yan.\n\nBut let's check if this is possible.\n\nIf Meng is host, the team can be Meng plus Xun or Meng plus Yan.\n\nBut Xun's statement says that he can be host only if Yan is a member. But here, Xun is a member, not the host. So, Xun's statement doesn't impose any restrictions.\n\nYan's statement: If Yan is host, she invites Zeng or Xun. But here, Yan isn't host, so no issue.\n\nZeng's statement: If Zeng is host, she invites Yan or Meng. But Zeng isn't host here, so no issue.\n\nSo, if Meng is host, the team can be Meng and Xun, or Meng and Yan.\n\nBut wait, let's check if this is possible.\n\nIf the team is Meng and Xun, then Xun is a member. From Xun's statement, he can be host only if Yan is a member. But here, Xun is a member, not the host, so that's fine.\n\nIf the team is Meng and Yan, then Yan is a member. From Yan's statement, if she were host, she would invite Zeng or Xun. But here, she's not host, so no issue.\n\nSo, both possibilities are acceptable.\n\nNow, let's summarize the possible teams:\n\n1. Yan Zi is host: Team is Yan and Zeng, or Yan and Xun.\n\n2. Zeng Yin is host: Team is Zeng and Yan.\n\n3. Xun Chen is host: Team is Xun and Yan.\n\n4. Meng Shen is host: Team is Meng and Xun, or Meng and Yan.\n\nNow, let's look at the options given:\n\nA) Meng shen, zeng yin\n\nB) Xun chen ,meng shen\n\nC) Zeng yin,xun chen\n\nD) Yan zi,meng shen\n\nWe need to see which of these combinations is impossible.\n\nLet's check each option.\n\nOption A: Meng and Zeng.\n\nIs this possible? From the above, when Meng is host, the team can be Meng and Xun or Meng and Yan. So, Meng and Zeng is not possible because Zeng isn't mentioned in Meng's team options. Also, Zeng's statement says that if she's host, she invites Yan or Meng. But here, Zeng is a member, not the host. So, Zeng's statement doesn't restrict her being a member if she's not host. However, the team would be Meng and Zeng, which includes Zeng as a member. But Zeng's statement doesn't say anything about non-host members, so it's possible. Wait, but let's check if this violates any other statements.\n\nIf the team is Meng and Zeng, then Meng is host, and Zeng is a member. From Meng's statement, members can only be Xun or Yan, but Zeng is not Xun or Yan, so this violates Meng's statement. Therefore, this combination is impossible.\n\nWait, that's a key point. If Meng is host, her statement says that members can only be Xun or Yan. So, if the team is Meng and Zeng, that's not allowed because Zeng is not Xun or Yan. Therefore, this combination is impossible.\n\nOption B: Xun and Meng.\n\nFrom the above, when Meng is host, the team can be Meng and Xun. So, this is possible.\n\nOption C: Zeng and Xun.\n\nIs this possible? Let's see. If Xun is host, the team must be Xun and Yan, not Zeng. So, Zeng can't be a member if Xun is host. Alternatively, if Zeng is host, the team would be Zeng and Yan or Zeng and Meng. But Zeng and Xun together would require that Xun is a member. However, if Zeng is host, she invites either Yan or Meng, not Xun. So, Zeng and Xun together is not possible.\n\nAlternatively, if Xun is host, the team is Xun and Yan, not Zeng. So, Zeng can't be a member if Xun is host. If Meng is host, the team is Meng and Xun or Meng and Yan. So, Zeng isn't a member there. If Yan is host, the team is Yan and Zeng or Yan and Xun. So, Zeng can be a member if Yan is host. But in that case, the team would be Yan and Zeng, not Xun and Zeng. So, Zeng and Xun together is only possible if Yan is host and she invites Xun, but then the team would be Yan and Xun, not Zeng. Therefore, Zeng and Xun together is impossible because if Xun is a member, the host must be Yan, but then Zeng can't be a member if Yan is host unless she's invited. Wait, no, if Yan is host and invites Xun, then the team is Yan and Xun. Zeng isn't a member. So, Zeng and Xun together is impossible because if Xun is a member, the host must be Yan, and Zeng isn't invited. Alternatively, if Zeng is host, she invites Meng or Yan, not Xun. So, Zeng and Xun together is impossible.\n\nWait, but let's think again. If the team is Zeng and Xun, who is the host? It can't be Zeng because Zeng's statement says she invites Yan or Meng, not Xun. So, if Zeng is host, she can't have Xun as a member. If Xun is host, he must have Yan as a member, so Xun and Yan. If Meng is host, she can have Xun or Yan, but not Zeng. If Yan is host, she can have Xun or Zeng. So, if Yan is host and invites Xun, the team is Yan and Xun. If Yan is host and invites Zeng, the team is Yan and Zeng. So, Zeng and Xun together is only possible if Yan is host and she invites both Xun and Zeng, but the team can only have two members, including the host. So, Yan can't invite both Xun and Zeng because that would make the team three people. Therefore, Zeng and Xun together is impossible.\n\nWait, but the team can have up to two members, including the host. So, if Yan is host, she can invite only one other member. So, she can't invite both Xun and Zeng. Therefore, Zeng and Xun together is impossible because it would require Yan to be the host and invite both, which isn't allowed.\n\nSo, option C is impossible.\n\nOption D: Yan and Meng.\n\nIs this possible? Let's see. If Meng is host, the team can be Meng and Xun or Meng and Yan. So, Yan and Meng together is possible if Meng is host and Yan is a member. But wait, from Xun's statement, if Xun is host, he must have Yan as a member. But here, Xun isn't the host, so that's fine. From Zeng's statement, if she's host, she invites Yan or Meng. But here, Meng is host, so Zeng's statement doesn't apply. From Yan's statement, if she's host, s