y deceive themselves. So this might be the assumption.\n\nC) A doctor is justified in deceaving a patient whenever doing so is likely to make the patient healthier.\nThis is about the justification of a doctor's actions. The context doesn't talk about justification, just that a third party provides the warning. So this might not be the assumption.\n\nD) Most of the techniques that help people quit smoking can also help people break other habits.\nThe context mentions that similar strategies can be used for other habits, so this seems to be a stated point, not an assumption. The argument is about the use of deception, not about the transferability of techniques.\n\nSo, putting it together, the argument relies on the idea that people can't easily deceive themselves, which is option B. Without that, the doctor's warning wouldn't be effective because the individuals wouldn't be able to convince themselves to quit smoking or break other habits on their own.\n"]
