      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out this logic problem. Let me read it again carefully.\n\nThe context is between Lance and Frank. Lance says that experience teaches us that every general rule has at least one exception. So, Lance is pointing out that no matter how many rules we have, there's always something that can break them. \n\nThen Frank responds. He says, \"What you conclude is itself a general rule.\" So, Frank is taking Lance's conclusion and saying that Lance's conclusion is a general rule. If we assume that Lance's conclusion is true, then there must be at least one general rule that has no exceptions. Because if every general rule has an exception, then Lance's conclusion would be that every rule has an exception, but if Lance's conclusion is itself a general rule, then it must have an exception, meaning there's at least one rule without an exception.\n\nFrank then concludes that Lance must withdraw his conclusion. So Frank is trying to counter Lance's argument by pointing out that Lance's own conclusion leads to a contradiction.\n\nThe question is asking: Based on the above information, Frank's argument is an attempt to counter Lance's conclusion by which option? The options are A, B, C, D.\n\nLet me break down the options:\n\nA) Demonstrating that Lance assumes the very thing he sets out to prove. Hmm, that sounds like a different type of logical fallacy, maybe the circular argument or assuming what you're trying to prove. But I'm not sure if that's what Frank is doing here.\n\nB) Establishing that experience teaches us the opposite of what Lance concludes. That doesn't seem right because Frank isn't saying that experience teaches the opposite; he's actually using Lance's own conclusion to argue against it.\n\nC) Showing that Lance's conclusion involves him in a contradiction. That sounds like what Frank is doing. He's using Lance's conclusion to show that it leads to a contradiction, meaning Lance's conclusion can't be true.\n\nD) Showing that it has no implications for any real cases. That doesn't fit because Frank is using the structure of the argument, not whether it applies to real cases.\n\nSo, putting it together, Frank is using Lance's conclusion to create a contradiction. Therefore, the answer should be C.\n</think>\n\nFrank's argument is an attempt to counter Lance's conclusion by showing that it leads to a contradiction.\n\n**Answer: C**",
