ait rights refer to something else, like the right to use a specific image, which might not be copyrightable. Or perhaps portrait rights are more about ownership of the image, which could be a separate category.\n\nAlternatively, maybe portrait rights are not considered intellectual property because they're more about ownership of a physical image rather than an intangible creation. But I'm not sure. Let me think again.\n\nWait, in the context given, intellectual property includes culture, art, etc., so any original work in those areas would be protected. Portraits are a form of art or culture, so their rights would fall under intellectual property. Therefore, C might actually be an intellectual property, which would mean the question is looking for something else.\n\nWait, but the options are A, B, C, D. I thought A, B, D are all intellectual properties. So maybe C is not. But I'm not entirely sure. Let me check each option again.\n\nA) Trademark right: Yes, it's an intellectual property.\n\nB) Right of invention: Yes, that's a patent, which is an intellectual property.\n\nC) Portrait rights: Hmm, if it's about the right to reproduce or use a portrait, that's more about copyright. But if it's about ownership of the image, maybe it's not. Alternatively, if it's about the right to use a portrait in a certain way, that's a derivative work, so it would be under copyright. So C might be an intellectual property.\n\nWait, but maybe portrait rights are not considered intellectual property because they're more about the physical image rather than an intangible creation. Or perhaps they are, because the image itself is a work of art. I'm a bit confused here.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the answer is C because portrait rights are not specifically covered under the definition provided, which mentions science, technology, culture, education, and art. So if portrait rights are more about personal use or ownership of a specific image, maybe they're not covered. But I'm not sure.\n\n