      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to figure out the answer to this logical reasoning question based on the given context. Let me read through the context carefully first.\n\nThe context talks about how people who silently read TV news and advertising program manuscripts can remember and recall more information than those who watch the TV program itself. That's interesting because it suggests that reading might be more effective than watching TV for retaining information.\n\nThen it says that if someone despises the value principle of a magazine, they can still read it with interest. But if this happens to a TV program, you can't watch it and change the channel quickly. So, the person can't switch channels easily, which might lead them to spend more time disagreeing with the views presented, which isn't ideal. The context mentions that we don't want to spend time on disagreeing views, so it's impossible to reflect, leading to fewer doubts about everything. \n\nIt also says that putting the two written records together makes it easy to find inconsistencies and can distinguish the difference of authority between information. Comparing two video reports is troublesome, and when asked to choose between two conflicting TV reports, the audience relies on intuition or prior beliefs rather than critical thinking.\n\nSo, putting this all together, the context is discussing how reading written materials, like TV scripts or magazine articles, can lead to better retention of information compared to watching TV. It also touches on how the way information is received (written vs. visual) affects people's behavior, their ability to reflect, and their decision-making when faced with conflicting information.\n\nNow, looking at the question: It asks what the paragraph is intended to illustrate. The options are A) Reading is more helpful than watching TV, B) The way of information reception affects behavior, C) TV makes people form wrong values, and D) People who like reading are calmer than those who watch TV.\n\nFrom the context, the main points are about the effectiveness of reading in retaining information, the impact on behavior when disagreeing, and the inconsistency in information when comparing video reports. However, the question is about what the paragraph is intended to illustrate, not just the points made.\n\nOption A is about reading being more helpful than watching TV, which is partially true but maybe not the main point. The context also discusses how reading can lead to fewer doubts and better reflection, which relates to behavior and decision-making.\n\nOption B talks about how the way information is received affects behavior. The context does discuss how written materials versus TV affects behavior, like spending time disagreeing and reflecting less. So this seems relevant.\n\nOption C suggests TV makes people form wrong values. The context mentions that if someone despises the value principle, they might read it with interest, but it doesn't specifically say TV makes wrong values. It's more about how people process information, not necessarily the values themselves.\n\nOption D is about people liking reading making them calmer. There's no direct evidence in the context about people's calmer nature; it's more about information retention and behavior related to disagreeing.\n\nSo, considering all this, the paragraph is illustrating how the method of information reception (reading vs. watching TV) affects people's behavior, their reflection, and their handling of conflicting information. Therefore, the best answer is B.\n</think>\n\nThe paragraph illustrates that the way information is received affects people's behavior. \n\nAnswer: B",
