e argument is about the number of planets that can support life, not the total number of planets.\n\nWait, the argument is using the ratio of 1/9 from Earth's solar system and applying it to the universe. So it's assuming that the proportion of Earth-like planets is the same across all systems. That's the key assumption here.\n\nLooking back at the options, option C says our solar system is similar to many others. If our solar system is unique, then the ratio might not apply. So the argument presumes that other systems are similar, which isn't stated.\n\nOption B is about if a planet is Earth-like, life will arise. The argument doesn't say that all Earth-like planets will have life, just that some will. So it's not necessarily assuming B, because it's not saying that every Earth-like planet will have life, just that the ratio implies that with many planets, some will.\n\nOption A is about different conditions. The argument doesn't specify whether conditions have to be exactly Earth-like or not. It's more about the ratio, so maybe A isn't the main assumption.\n\nSo, the main assumption is that other planetary systems are similar to ours in terms of having Earth-like planets. That's option C.\n</think>\n\nThe argument presumes that our solar system is similar to many others in the universe, allowing the ratio of Earth-like planets to be applied universally. This is option C.\n\nAnswer: C",
