distributed. If the blame is spread across all parties, it's harder for any one party to be targeted, so incumbents stay. But if the blame is concentrated on one party, that party's incumbents are more likely to be defeated.\n\nLooking at the options:\n\nA) Talks about whether incumbents should be voted out depending on their challengers. That seems relevant because if the party has strong challengers, maybe they get defeated even if the scandal is on all parties. But I'm not sure if this is the main principle.\n\nB) Compares the accuracy of blame judgments. If the scandal is blamed on all, it's more accurate than blaming one party. But I don't think the context is about accuracy; it's about re-election outcomes.\n\nC) States that when scandals are less the responsibility of individual incumbents but parties, then the responsible party must be penalized. This seems to align with the idea that when the party is blamed, they lose. So, penalizing the party makes sense.\n\nD) Suggests that incumbents should not seek re-election if they're rightly blamed. But the context says they do seek re-election, so this might not fit.\n\nWait, the context says that the proportion of incumbents seeking re-election is high and constant. So, they do seek re-election regardless of the blame. So, D might not be the right answer because it's suggesting they shouldn't seek re-election if blamed, which contradicts the context.\n\nSo, going back, the principle is about the distribution of blame. When the scandal is blamed on all parties, it's less likely to target any one party, so incumbents stay. When it's blamed on one party, that party's incumbents are targeted.\n\nSo, the principle is that when a party is blamed, they should be penalized. That's option C.\n\nWait, but option A says that whether incumbents should be re-elected depends on their challengers. So, if a party has strong challengers, maybe they get defeated even if the scandal is on all parties. But the context doesn't mention anything about challengers' strength, just that when the scandal is on one party, they lose.\n\nSo, maybe the main principle is that when a party is blamed, they should be penalized, which is option C.\n\nAlternatively, option A is about the decision to re-elect depending on the challenger. But the context doesn't specify that; it's more about the distribution of blame leading to different outcomes.\n\nI think the best answer is C because it directly addresses the responsibility of the party being penalized when they're blamed, which explains why when the scandal is on one party, they lose.\n"] 70%|██████▉   | 418/600 [4:31:51<18:26,  6.08s/it]
