final_output:  ['\n\nThe incorrect statement is C) There must be other criterion to test the truth.\n\n**Answer: C**', '\nOkay, so I\'m trying to solve this logical reasoning problem. Let me read it carefully.\n\nThe context given is: "Practice is the sole criterion for testing truth." \n\nThe question is asking which of the statements A, B, C, or D is incorrect based on this context.\n\nFirst, I need to understand what each option is saying.\n\nOption A: "Practice can test the truth." That sounds correct because the context says practice is the sole criterion, so it can test truth.\n\nOption B: "Only practice can test the truth." Hmm, this is similar to the context but phrased differently. The context says practice is the sole criterion, which implies that only practice can test truth. So B seems correct too.\n\nOption C: "There must be other criterion to test the truth." Wait, the context says practice is the sole criterion, meaning nothing else can test truth. So this statement is saying the opposite. It\'s incorrect because it suggests there are other criteria, which isn\'t true according to the context.\n\nOption D: "No other criterion can test the truth." Yes, this aligns with the context. Since practice is the sole criterion, no other methods can test truth. So D is correct.\n\nSo, the question is asking which is incorrect. From my analysis, A and B are correct, C is incorrect, and D is correct. Therefore, the incorrect statement is C.\n']
