      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to solve this logical reasoning problem. Let me read through the context and the question carefully.\n\nThe context says that in Berinia, the legal drinking age was raised from 18 to 21 in 1990. The reason was to reduce alcohol consumption and thus reduce alcohol-related traffic deaths among people under 21. Since 1990, alcohol-related traffic deaths among people under 21 have decreased significantly. However, surveys show that people in that age group are drinking just as much alcohol as they did before 1990.\n\nThe question is asking which of the options A, B, C, or D, if true, would most help to resolve the apparent discrepancy. The discrepancy here is that even though there's been a decrease in deaths, the amount of alcohol consumed by under-21s hasn't gone down.\n\nLet me break this down. The key points are:\n\n1. Legal drinking age increased to 21 in 1990.\n2. Alcohol-related traffic deaths among under 21s decreased significantly.\n3. Surveys show that under-21s are drinking as much as before 1990.\n\nSo, the problem is that even though the age of legal drinking was raised, the amount of alcohol consumed by the under-21s hasn't decreased. But the number of deaths has decreased. How can that be?\n\nI think the answer lies in understanding why the number of deaths has decreased despite the same amount of alcohol being consumed. Maybe more people are getting intoxicated because they're drinking more, but that doesn't make sense because the surveys say they're drinking the same amount. Alternatively, maybe the under-21s are drinking less in terms of quantity but more in terms of frequency, but the surveys measure total consumption, not frequency.\n\nWait, no, the surveys show that the amount of alcohol consumed is the same as before. So, perhaps the decrease in deaths is because the population under 21 has changed. For example, maybe there are fewer young people now, so even though the same number of people are drinking, the overall impact on traffic deaths is less. But that's not one of the options.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the under-21s are now drinking less in terms of alcohol consumed per person, but the surveys are measuring total consumption. Wait, no, the context says they're drinking just as much as before, so total consumption hasn't gone down.\n\nWait, perhaps the under-21s are now drinking less in terms of the amount per drink, but the number of drinks they have hasn't increased. Or maybe they're getting less drunk, so fewer accidents. But that's not directly related to the options.\n\nLooking at the options:\n\nA) Alcohol-related traffic deaths among people over 21 have increased slightly since 1990.\n\nHmm, this is about deaths in people over 21. The question is about under-21s. So this might not directly explain why under-21s' deaths have decreased while their alcohol consumption hasn't.\n\nB) For the population as a whole, annual alcohol consumption is no lower now than it was in 1990.\n\nThis says that overall, people are drinking as much or more. But the under-21s are drinking the same as before. So, if overall consumption is the same, and under-21s are drinking the same, then the rest of the population (21 and above) must be drinking less. But how does that help explain the decrease in under-21 deaths? Maybe because the under-21s are drinking the same amount but have fewer accidents, perhaps because they're more cautious or the roads are safer. But I'm not sure if this directly addresses the discrepancy.\n\nC) The proportion of people under 21 who own a car is higher now than it was in 1990.\n\nIf more under-21s own cars, maybe they're driving more, leading to more accidents. But the deaths have decreased, so perhaps they're driving less, or perhaps the cars are safer. But this doesn't directly explain why alcohol consumption hasn't gone down but deaths have.\n\nD) Alcohol consumption away from home, for example in bars and restaurants, is much lower among people under 21 than it was in 1990.\n\nThis suggests that under-21s are drinking less in bars and restaurants, which are places where alcohol is consumed away from home. If they're drinking less in those places, maybe they're drinking more at home, but the surveys measure total consumption. Wait, but the surveys show that total alcohol consumption hasn't gone down. So if they're drinking less away from home, perhaps they're drinking more at home, but that doesn't necessarily explain why the number of deaths has decreased.\n\nWait, maybe the key is that the legal drinking age was raised, so under-21s are now drinking less because they can't get alcohol legally. But the context says that the surveys show they're drinking as much as before. So that doesn't make sense.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the under-21s are now drinking less in terms of the amount consumed, but the surveys are measuring total consumption, so that's not it.\n\nWait, perhaps the under-21s are now drinking less in terms of the number of drinks per session, but the surveys measure total consumption, so that's not it.\n\nWait, maybe the under-21s are now drinking less in terms of the amount consumed per person, but the surveys are measuring total consumption, so that's not it.\n\nWait, perhaps the under-21s are now drinking less in terms of the number of drinks per day, but the surveys measure total consumption, so that's not it.\n\nWait, maybe the under-21s are now drinking less in terms of the amount consumed per person, but the surveys are measuring total consumption, so that's not it.\n\nWait, perhaps the under-21s are now drinking less in terms of the amount consumed per person, but the surveys are measuring total consumption, so that's not it.\n\nWait, maybe the under-21s are now drinking less in terms of the amount consumed per person, but the surveys are measuring total consumption, so that's not it.\n\nWait, perhaps the under-21s are now drinking less in terms of the amount consumed per person, but the 