      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to solve this logical reasoning problem. Let me read through the context and the question carefully.\n\nThe context says that in Berinia, the legal drinking age was raised from 18 to 21 in 1990. The reason was to reduce alcohol consumption and thus reduce alcohol-related traffic deaths among people under 21. Since 1990, alcohol-related traffic deaths among people under 21 have decreased significantly. However, surveys show that people in that age group are drinking just as much alcohol as they did before 1990.\n\nThe question is asking which of the options A, B, C, or D, if true, would most help to resolve the apparent discrepancy. The discrepancy here is that even though there's been a decrease in deaths, the amount of alcohol consumed by under-21s hasn't gone down.\n\nLet me break this down. The key points are:\n\n1. Legal drinking age increased to 21 in 1990.\n2. Alcohol-related traffic deaths among under 21s decreased significantly.\n3. Surveys show that under-21s are drinking as much as before 1990.\n\nSo, the problem is that even though the age of legal drinking was raised, the amount of alcohol consumed by the under-21s hasn't decreased. But the number of deaths has decreased. How can that be?\n\nI think the answer lies in understanding why the number of deaths has decreased despite the same amount of alcohol being consumed. Maybe more people are getting intoxicated because they're drinking more, but that doesn't make sense because the surveys say they're drinking the same amount. Alternatively, maybe the under-21s are drinking less in terms of quantity but more in terms of frequency, but the surveys measure total consumption, not frequency.\n\nWait, no, the surveys show that the amount of alcohol consumed is the same as before. So, perhaps the decrease in deaths is because the population under 21 has changed. For example, maybe there are fewer young people now, so even though the same number of people are drinking,