nder 21 than it was in 1990.\n\nThis suggests that under-21s are drinking less in bars and restaurants, which are places where alcohol is consumed away from home. If they're drinking less in those places, maybe they're drinking more at home, but the surveys measure total consumption. Wait, but the surveys show that total alcohol consumption hasn't gone down. So if they're drinking less away from home, perhaps they're drinking more at home, but that doesn't necessarily explain why the number of deaths has decreased.\n\nWait, maybe the key is that the legal drinking age was raised, so under-21s are now drinking less because they can't get alcohol legally. But the context says that the surveys show they're drinking as much as before. So that doesn't make sense.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the under-21s are now drinking less in terms of the amount consumed, but the surveys are measuring total consumption, so that's not it.\n\nWait, perhaps the under-21s are now drinking less in terms of the number of drinks per session, but the surveys measure total consumption, so that's not it.\n\nWait, maybe the under-21s are now drinking less in terms of the amount consumed per person, but the surveys are measuring total consumption, so that's not it.\n\nWait, perhaps the under-21s are now drinking less in terms of the number of drinks per day, but the surveys measure total consumption, so that's not it.\n\nWait, maybe the under-21s are now drinking less in terms of the amount consumed per person, but the surveys are measuring total consumption, so that's not it.\n\nWait, perhaps the under-21s are now drinking less in terms of the amount consumed per person, but the surveys are measuring total consumption, so that's not it.\n\nWait, maybe the under-21s are now drinking less in terms of the amount consumed per person, but the surveys are measuring total consumption, so that's not it.\n\nWait, perhaps the under-21s are now drinking less in terms of the amount consumed per person, but the 