y 100 unless they're also divisible by 400. So, roughly, every four years, there's an extra day, causing the shift. Without leap years, the shift would be one day each year. With leap years, it's two days every four years, which averages to 0.5 days per year shift.\n\nIn the proposed system, the extra day is added every four years, but it's not part of any week. So, the shift would be different. Instead of adding a day every four years, maybe the shift is adjusted so that the days of the week realign in a way that January 1 is always a Sunday. So, perhaps the shift is two days every four years, making the total shift over four years equivalent to two days, so that after four years, the shift is two days, and after eight years, four days, etc.\n\nWait, I'm getting confused. Let me try to think of it differently. If January 1 is always a Sunday, then each year, the same date would fall two days later in the week. So, if January 1 is Sunday this year, next year it would be Tuesday, then Thursday, etc. But that doesn't make sense because the current system shifts one day each year. So, maybe the proposal is to adjust the shift so that it's two days every four years, making the total shift over four years equivalent to two days, so that the days of the week realign every eight years or something.\n\nI think I'm overcomplicating it. Let me try to focus on the question: which group would continue to have scheduling conflicts if the proposal is implemented.\n\nThe key point is that with the current system, the days shift each year, causing anniversaries to fall on different days, leading to scheduling conflicts. The proposal aims to make the days shift in a way that January 1 is always a Sunday, so maybe the shift is adjusted to make the days of the week realign in a different cycle.\n\nBut the question is about which group would continue to have scheduling conflicts. So, let's look at the options again.\n\nOption B: People with anniversaries on December 30 or 31. If the ex