\nI don't think this is necessarily an assumption. The method doesn't specify anything about warm vs. cold; it's more about using the existing tolerances regardless of temperature extremes.\n\nC) The process of dating is more accurate for beetles than for other organisms.\n\nThe context mentions that they used dating on beetles, but it doesn't say anything about the dating process being more accurate. The method focuses on using the same period for multiple species, so this might not be an assumption.\n\nD) Fossils of different species found in the same place belonged to different periods.\n\nNo, the opposite is true. They found that several species from the same place had fossils from the same period, so this option is the opposite of what's stated.\n\nSo, putting it all together, the researchers must assume that the temperature tolerances of the beetles haven't changed much over the 22,000 years. That's option A.\n"]
