s than boys in S (100). So D is correct.\n\nWait, but earlier I had a case where D was incorrect. So maybe I made a mistake in that earlier example.\n\nWait, in the second example, I had:\n\nS1:30 boys,10 girls\nS2:30 boys,10 girls\nLA:41 boys,79 girls\n\nTotal boys:30+30+41=101\nTotal girls:10+10+79=99\n\nSo girls in LA:79, boys in S:60. So 60<79, making D incorrect.\n\nBut in this case, the total girls are 99, which is less than boys (101). So the conditions are met, but D is incorrect.\n\nWait, but in this case, girls in LA are 79, which is more than boys in S (60). So D is incorrect.\n\nTherefore, D is not necessarily correct.\n\nWait, but in the first example, D was correct, but in the second, it's not. So D is not necessarily correct.\n\nWait, but the question is asking which must be correct. So if in some cases it's correct and in others not, then D is not necessarily correct.\n\nWait, but maybe I'm missing something. Let me think again.\n\nThe key is that there are more science classes than LA classes, and more boys than girls overall.\n\nSo, if there are more science classes, and more boys overall, then it's possible that the number of boys in science classes is more than the number of girls in LA classes.\n\nBut in the second example, that's not the case.\n\nWait, but maybe the way I distributed the girls in LA is not possible. Because if there are more science classes, and more boys overall, then perhaps the girls in LA can't be that high.\n\nWait, in the second example, girls in LA are 79, which is almost all the girls. But since there are more science classes, maybe the girls in LA can't be that high because the science classes have more students.\n\nWait, let me recalculate.\n\nIn the second example:\n\nS1:30 boys,10 girls\nS2:30 boys,10 girls\nLA:41 boys,79 girls\n\nTotal students:30+10+30+10+41+79=200\n\nTotal boys:101, girls:99.\n\nBut in this case, girls in LA are 79, which is almost all the girls. But since there are more science classes, maybe th