 cost is higher for canned. So, maybe the cost per ounce is higher for canned? Or maybe not. I'm not sure. Let me think again.\n\nWait, the total cost per day for canned is higher, but they eat fewer ounces. So, if you have to buy less canned food each day, but each ounce is more calorie-dense, but the cost per day is higher. So, the cost per ounce might be higher or lower. The context doesn't specify, so I can't conclude A for sure. It's possible, but not necessarily supported.\n\nB) On average, it costs no less to feed a cat that eats fewer ounces of food per day than it does to feed a cat that eats more ounces of food per day.\nWait, the context says that feeding canned (which makes the cat eat fewer ounces) typically costs more per day than feeding dry (which makes the cat eat more ounces). So, the cost per day is higher for the cat that eats fewer ounces. So, this statement is saying that it's no less, meaning it's equal or more. But according to the context, it's more. So, this statement is actually supported because feeding the cat that eats fewer ounces (canned) costs more per day than feeding the one that eats more (dry). So, B seems correct.\n\nC) On average, cats fed canned cat food eat more calories per day than those fed dry cat food.\nWait, the context says that canned has more calories per ounce, but the cat eats fewer ounces. So, total calories would be fewer. For example, if canned is 10 oz with 500 calories per oz, that's 5000 calories. Dry might be 20 oz with 300 calories per oz, that's 6000 calories. So, the canned cat eats fewer calories per day. So, C is incorrect.\n\nD) Typically, cats are fed either canned cat food or dry cat food, or both.\nThe context doesn't mention anything about how cats are typically fed. It just compares the two types. So, D is not supported by the information given.\n\nSo, the correct answer should be B.\n"] 78%|███████▊  | 467/600 [5:33:32<45:31, 20.54s/it]
