ent.\n\nC) The chemical structure of synthetic pesticides is usually simpler than that of natural drugs contained in plants.\n\nIf synthetic pesticides are simpler, they might be more effective at targeting specific organisms, which could mean they're more harmful. This would undermine the argument because it suggests that synthetic pesticides could be more dangerous than the natural drugs. So this would weaken the argument.\n\nD) Natural drugs contained in plants are usually only suitable for resisting specific organisms, while synthetic pesticides are usually harmful to a variety of organisms.\n\nThis also suggests that synthetic pesticides are more dangerous because they affect more organisms. So this would undermine the argument.\n\nSo the options that do not undermine the argument are A and B. The question is asking which one does not undermine, so either A or B.\n\nBut the question is which statement, if true, does not undermine the argument. So the correct answer would be the one that supports the argument.\n\nOption A says the concentration of natural drugs is much lower than synthetic pesticides. If that's true, then even though natural drugs are present, the amount ingested is less, making synthetic pesticides less harmful. This supports the argument, so it doesn't undermine it.\n\nOption B says humans have adapted to natural drugs for thousands of years, which supports the argument because it explains why synthetic pesticides aren't harmful. So it doesn't undermine the argument.\n\nWait, but the question is which does not undermine the argument. So both A and B support the argument, meaning they don't undermine it. But the options are A, B, C, D, and the question is which one does not undermine. So the correct answer would be either A or B.\n\nBut looking back, the question is asking which statement, if true, does not undermine the argument. So the correct answer is the one that supports the argument.\n\nOption A: If natural drugs have lower concentration