cause it shows that cancer rates are the same, so irradiation doesn't cause cancer.\n\nSo, all options seem to weaken the argument. But the question says that each, if true, weakens except one. So, perhaps I'm wrong about one of them.\n\nWait, maybe option B is not directly related. Let me think again. Option B says that cancer has many causes unrelated to radiation. So, if that's true, then the advocate's specific claim that radiation causes cancer via those products is still possible, but the general argument that radiation causes cancer is not necessarily weakened. Because the advocate's argument is about the specific cause (radiolytic products), not that radiation in general causes cancer.\n\nWait, but the advocate's argument is that irradiation causes cancer because of those products. So, if B is true, it means that cancer can be caused by other factors, so the advocate's specific claim is weakened, but the general argument about radiation causing cancer is still possible. So, B does weaken the advocate's argument.\n\nHmm, maybe I'm missing something else. Let me think about the structure of the argument.\n\nThe advocate's argument is that irradiation is bad because of three reasons: exposure to radiation, reduced vitamins, and radiolytic products causing cancer.\n\nIf any of these reasons are weakened, the argument is weakened.\n\nOption A: Weakens the third reason.\n\nOption B: Weakens the third reason because it shows that the cause (radiation) isn't the only one.\n\nOption C: Weakens the second reason.\n\nOption D: Weakens the third reason because it shows that the third reason (radiolytic products causing cancer) isn't valid because cancer rates are the same.\n\nWait, but maybe option B is not directly related to the third reason. It's more about the cause of cancer in general. So, perhaps option B doesn't directly weaken the advocate's specific argument about the radiolytic products causing cancer.\n\nWait, but the advocate's argument is that the radiolyt