sition.\n\nOption C: \"Celestial bodies in the universe can be divided into natural and man-made celestial bodies.\" This is categorizing celestial bodies into two broader categories: natural and man-made. So this is more about classification, not decomposition. The context mentions dividing concepts into species, which are more specific categories. So this might be an example of division into species, but the question is about decomposition, which is breaking into parts.\n\nOption D: \"The penalty is divided into principal punishment, deprivation of political rights, confiscation of property, etc.\" Penalties are being broken down into different types of punishments. This is again categorizing penalties into specific types, which is similar to division into species. It's not about breaking an object into parts but classifying different aspects of a concept.\n\nSo, the question is about decomposition, which is breaking an object into parts without creating new species. Option B is about breaking a city into districts, which are parts of the city, not species. So B seems to fit decomposition.\n\nWait, but the context also mentions division as categorizing concepts into species. So both B and C are examples of division, but B is decomposition (breaking into parts) and C is division into categories (species). The question is asking which is correctly divided according to the definition, which includes both methods. But the question is about which is correctly divided, so both B and C are correct in their own ways, but the question is about decomposition.\n\nWait, the question is asking which is correctly divided based on the definition provided. The definition includes both division of concepts into species and decomposition of objects into parts. So both B and C are correct, but the options are A, B, C, D, and only one is correct.\n\nWait, the question is about which is correctly divided, so both B and C are correct, but the options are separate. So perhaps the questi