t get pollution. So, D might not be a valid conclusion because it\'s assuming that environmental damage is solely due to economic development, which isn\'t stated.\n\nOption A says inevitable stagnation or ecological damage. But the context doesn\'t say it\'s inevitable. They can choose to balance both, so A might not be correct.\n\nOption B suggests stopping all industry, which the context doesn\'t recommend because it says development can\'t be avoided, but it doesn\'t say they should stop all of it.\n\nOption C is incorrect because it says quality of life depends only on economy and environment, which isn\'t supported.\n\nSo, perhaps none of the options are correct, but that can\'t be. Maybe I\'m missing something.\n\nWait, the context says that preserving and developing traditional industries is necessary for economic development, which can\'t be replaced by tourism. So, economic development is important, but they have to protect the environment as well. So, if they don\'t develop enough, they might get pollution. So, if the environment is damaged, it\'s because they didn\'t develop enough, which is option D.\n\nBut I\'m not sure if that\'s the right way to interpret it. Maybe the answer is D because it\'s the only one that makes sense based on the context, even if it\'s a bit of a stretch.\n']
