r. Again, this is about what happens when the typhoon is passing, not about the mechanism of the spraying. Maybe relevant, but not directly a prerequisite for the argument.\n\nD) Driving force of typhoon comes from heat generated by sunlight on sea water. The context mentions that the reflection of clouds increases, which is linked to the energy the typhoon gets. So if the driving force is from sunlight heating the sea water, then spraying droplets would affect that. But wait, the argument is about the reflection preventing the typhoon from moving. So maybe D is more about why the typhoon forms in the first place, not directly about the reflection method.\n\nWait, the argument is that spraying droplets increases reflection, which stops the typhoon. So the conclusion is that this method works. But for that to be true, we need to assume that the reflection actually happens. So maybe A is necessary because if the droplets can't regroup, they can't reflect effectively. So A is a prerequisite.\n\nAlternatively, D says the driving force is from sunlight. If that's true, then the reflection method works because it's based on sunlight. But the argument doesn't directly link the reflection to the driving force. It just says that increasing reflection stops the typhoon. So maybe D isn't necessary because the argument doesn't depend on the driving force being from sunlight; it's just about the reflection.\n\nWait, but the argument is about the effect of reflection on the typhoon's movement. So if the reflection doesn't happen because the droplets can't regroup, then the conclusion that the typhoon won't move is defeated. So A is necessary because without the droplets regrouping, the reflection can't occur, so the method wouldn't work.\n\nSo I think A is the correct answer.\n"]
