hat explains why they have trouble finding jobs. It might not directly address their employability issues.\n\nB) Most returnees are not familiar with the domestic employment situation and enterprise needs.\nThis seems relevant. If returnees don\'t know about the job market in China, they might not know what skills or qualifications are needed. That could make it harder for them to find jobs, which fits with the context where they believe they have disadvantages.\n\nC) The actual salary levels of most returnees do not meet their expectations.\nThis is about salary expectations. While salary is important, the context doesn\'t mention anything about salary being a problem. The main issue is finding a job, not necessarily not getting a good salary. So maybe this isn\'t the best fit.\n\nD) Graduates of famous overseas universities such as Oxford are still favored by employers.\nThis is about the quality of the education. It suggests that even though some returnees come from prestigious universities, employers still prefer them. But the context doesn\'t specify that only these universities are studied, and it doesn\'t say anything about the quality of the returnees\' education. So this might not directly explain why all returnees have trouble finding jobs.\n\nPutting it all together, option B seems to directly address the lack of knowledge about the job market, which would make it harder for them to find jobs. The other options either don\'t address the main issue or are about different aspects like salary or education quality.\n']
