ly virtuous, not that it requires rejecting material things.\n\nB) People who approve of their own character and projects tend to lead morally virtuous lives.\nThis seems to align with the argument. The argument is saying that genuine happiness is approval, and that leads to a good life, which is morally virtuous. So, this is probably the assumption.\n\nC) Attaining happiness is the real goal of people who strive for material well-being.\nThe context doesn't mention anything about the real goal of people striving for material well-being. It's more about how happiness is defined, not about goals.\n\nD) Approval of one's own character and projects tends not to result in pleasurable feelings.\nThe context says that happiness isn't about pleasurable feelings but about approval. So, the argument assumes that approval doesn't lead to pleasurable feelings. But is this an assumption? The argument is about the definition of happiness, not necessarily about the feelings resulting from approval. So maybe this isn't the main assumption.\n\nWait, but the argument is saying that genuine happiness is about approval, not about feelings. So, perhaps the assumption is that approval doesn't lead to pleasurable feelings, which would make D an assumption. But I'm not sure if that's the main point.\n\nAlternatively, the main point is that approval leads to a morally virtuous life, which is the assumption in B. So, I think B is the better answer.\n\nI'm a bit confused between B and D. Let me think again. The argument is about the definition of happiness and the nature of a good life. It's connecting approval to moral virtue. So, the assumption is that approval leads to a morally virtuous life, which is option B.\n\nD is about feelings, which might be a consequence, but the argument doesn't directly assume that approval doesn't lead to pleasurable feelings. It's more about defining happiness as approval, not about the feelings themselves.\n\nSo, I think B is the correct answer.\n"] 83%|██