 broadened the duties of the planning department.\n\nIf the duties were broadened, that could mean that the same budget is covering more responsibilities, which might make the spending seem more efficient. But does it necessarily mean that the spending is five times as much? Maybe, but I'm not sure if this directly supports the claim.\n\nB) Departments other than the planning department have had much larger budget increases since 2001.\n\nThis option suggests that other departments have had much bigger increases. If other departments have much larger increases, then the Planning Department's increase might not seem as significant in comparison. However, the editorial is comparing the Planning Department's increase to the same duties, so if other departments have larger increases, it might mean that the Planning Department's increase isn't as big in relative terms. But I'm not sure if this directly supports the claim that the spending is five times as much.\n\nC) The budget figures used in the original editorial were adjusted for inflation.\n\nIf the budget figures were adjusted for inflation, that would mean that the figures are in real terms, not nominal. If they weren't adjusted, the increase might be misleading. For example, if prices went up, the nominal increase could be due to inflation, not actual spending increase. So, if the figures are adjusted, it would show the real spending increase. This would support the claim because it would mean that the actual spending has increased by five times, not just the budget.\n\nD) In some years between 2001 and this year, the planning department budget did not increase.\n\nThis option suggests that the budget didn't increase every year, but the overall increase from 2001 to now is a factor of 5.24. However, this doesn't directly address whether the spending per duty has increased five times. It just shows that the budget didn't increase every year, which might be a point against the claim, but it doesn't support it.\n\nSo, considering all options, option C seems the most relevant because adjusting for inflation would provide a clearer picture of the actual spending increase, thereby supporting the editorial's conclusion.\n"]
