't believe the treatment will work and their condition worsens, that might be because their expectations are negative, which could be part of the explanation. So this supports the idea that expectations matter.\n\nOption D: "The subject was injected with saline and convinced that it was a painkiller, and the subject\'s brain then secreted the painkiller endorphin." This is a classic example of the placebo effect. The patient\'s belief that the saline is a painkiller (despite it being saline) triggers the release of endorphins, which provide relief. This directly supports the explanation given in the context.\n\nSo, going back to Option B, if someone can\'t anticipate the future, their expectations are static, so the placebo effect (which relies on changing expectations) wouldn\'t work on them. Therefore, the placebo wouldn\'t have an effect, which contradicts the explanation. So Option B does not support the explanation.\n']
