bout the possibility of some being hired, not the impossibility of all.\n\nB) In this recruitment examination, there may be candidates who will not be hired.\nThis seems closer. If there may be candidates who won\'t be hired, that directly supports the idea that not everyone can get a position. It\'s a possibility that aligns with the conclusion.\n\nC) In this recruitment examination, there are bound to be candidates who will not be hired.\nThis is similar to B but uses "bound to be" which is stronger. It\'s stating that it\'s certain that some won\'t be hired. But the context only says it\'s impossible for all to be hired, not that some must be. So, maybe this is too strong.\n\nD) In this recruitment examination, there must be candidates to be hired.\nThis seems off. The context is about not being able to hire all, not about needing to hire some. So D doesn\'t make sense in this context.\n\nWait, let me think again. The conclusion is that it\'s impossible for all candidates to be hired. So, the reasoning must be that because there are limited positions, some candidates can\'t get hired. So, the reasoning that leads to this conclusion would be that there are not enough positions for everyone, meaning some will be left out.\n\nSo, option C says "there are bound to be candidates who will not be hired." That directly supports the conclusion because it\'s stating that some candidates won\'t get hired, which is exactly what the conclusion is about.\n\nOption B says "there may be candidates who will not be hired." That\'s also possible, but it\'s a weaker statement. The context implies that it\'s impossible for all to be hired, which means at least some won\'t be. So, C is more precise and directly leads to the conclusion.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer should be C.\n']
