      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to solve this logical reasoning problem. Let me read through the context and the question carefully.\n\nThe university administrator made a statement about funding a new department. The key points are:\n\n1. A proposal for a new department won't be funded if two conditions are met:\n   a. There are fewer than 50 people per year available for hire in that field.\n   b. The proposed department duplicates more than 25% of the material covered in one existing department.\n\n2. The proposed Area Studies Department duplicates more than 25% of the material in the existing Anthropology Department.\n\n3. Despite this duplication, the university will fund the new department.\n\nThe question is asking which statement logically follows from these statements. The options are A, B, C, D.\n\nLet me break down the context step by step.\n\nFirst, the funding condition is that a new department won't be funded if both conditions a and b are true. So, for a department to be funded, at least one of these conditions must be false.\n\nIn logical terms, the funding condition is: If (fewer than 50 hires) AND (duplicates >25% of an existing dept), then not funded.\n\nSo, the contrapositive would be: If funded, then it's not the case that both (fewer than 50) and (duplicates >25%). Which means, either there are at least 50 hires, or the duplication is <=25%, or both.\n\nBut in this case, the university is funding the new department despite the duplication. So, the fact that the duplication is more than 25% (since it duplicates more than 25% of Anthropology) means that the other condition must be false for funding to occur.\n\nTherefore, the number of people per year available for hire in Area Studies must be at least 50. Because if there were fewer than 50, and duplication was more than 25%, the department wouldn't be funded. But since it is funded, at least one of the conditions must fail. Since duplication is more than 25%, the other condition (hires <50) must be false, meaning hires >=50.\n\nNow, looking at the options:\n\nA) If the proposed area studies department did not duplicate more than 25% of the material covered in anthropology, then the new department would not be funded.\n\nWait, but the context says that the duplication is more than 25% of Anthropology. So, if it didn't duplicate more than 25%, would it not be funded? Let's see. The funding condition is that if both conditions are met, it's not funded. So, if duplication is <=25%, then even if hires are <50, it's not funded. But if duplication is <=25%, the department would be funded only if hires >=50. But the context doesn't say anything about what would happen if duplication is <=25%. The context only talks about the case where duplication is more than 25%. So, A is making a statement that if duplication is <=25%, then not funded. But that's not necessarily true because the funding condition is only triggered if both conditions are met. So, if duplication is <=25%, even if hires are <50, it would be funded. So, A is incorrect because it's assuming that if duplication is <=25%, then it's not funded, which isn't necessarily true.\n\nB) The proposed area studies department would not duplicate more than 25 percent of the material covered in any existing department other than anthropology.\n\nThe context only mentions that it duplicates more than 25% of Anthropology. It doesn't say anything about other departments. So, B is making a broader claim that it doesn't duplicate more than 25% in any other department, but the context doesn't provide information about other departments. So, B is not necessarily true.\n\nC) The field of area studies has fewer than 50 people per year available for hire.\n\nBut from the context, we concluded that the number of hires must be at least 50 because the duplication is more than 25%, so the department is funded. Therefore, C is incorrect because it's the opposite of what we concluded.\n\nD) The field of area studies has at least 50 people per year available for hire.\n\nYes, that's exactly what we concluded. Since the duplication is more than 25%, the only way the department is funded is if the number of hires is at least 50. So, D is correct.\n\nLet me double-check. The key is that the department is funded, so the condition that would have made it unfunded (fewer than 50 and more than 25% duplication) is not met. Since duplication is more than 25%, the other condition must fail, meaning at least 50 hires. So, D is the correct answer.\n</think>\n\nThe correct answer is D.\n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**\n\n1. **Understanding the Funding Condition:**\n   - A new department is not funded if:\n     - (A) Fewer than 50 people per year are hired.\n     - (B) The department duplicates more than 25% of an existing department's material.\n\n2. **Given Information:**\n   - The proposed Area Studies Department duplicates more than 25% of the material from Anthropology.\n   - Despite this duplication, the university funds the department.\n\n3. **Logical Deduction:**\n   - Since the department is funded, the condition that would have prevented funding (both A and B being true) must not hold.\n   - Because B is true (it duplicates more than 25%), A must be false. Therefore, the number of hires must be at least 50.\n\n4. **Evaluating Options:**\n   - **A:** Incorrect. The context doesn't specify what happens if duplication is \u226425%; it only states the condition for when duplication is >25%.\n   - **B:** Incorrect. The context doesn't provide information about duplication in other departments.\n   - **C:** Incorrect. The opposite of what's concluded; the number of hires is at least 50.\n   - **D:** Correct. The number of hires is at least 50.\n\n**Answer: D**",
