ore than 25%. So, A is making a statement that if duplication is <=25%, then not funded. But that's not necessarily true because the funding condition is only triggered if both conditions are met. So, if duplication is <=25%, even if hires are <50, it would be funded. So, A is incorrect because it's assuming that if duplication is <=25%, then it's not funded, which isn't necessarily true.\n\nB) The proposed area studies department would not duplicate more than 25 percent of the material covered in any existing department other than anthropology.\n\nThe context only mentions that it duplicates more than 25% of Anthropology. It doesn't say anything about other departments. So, B is making a broader claim that it doesn't duplicate more than 25% in any other department, but the context doesn't provide information about other departments. So, B is not necessarily true.\n\nC) The field of area studies has fewer than 50 people per year available for hire.\n\nBut from the context, we concluded that the number of hires must be at least 50 because the duplication is more than 25%, so the department is funded. Therefore, C is incorrect because it's the opposite of what we concluded.\n\nD) The field of area studies has at least 50 people per year available for hire.\n\nYes, that's exactly what we concluded. Since the duplication is more than 25%, the only way the department is funded is if the number of hires is at least 50. So, D is correct.\n\nLet me double-check. The key is that the department is funded, so the condition that would have made it unfunded (fewer than 50 and more than 25% duplication) is not met. Since duplication is more than 25%, the other condition must fail, meaning at least 50 hires. So, D is the correct answer.\n"] 88%|████████▊ | 530/600 [6:05:27<11:03,  9.48s/it]
