t every idea is a variation. So, maybe this is not necessarily true because even average discoverers could be making variations, but the option is too broad in saying "all great ones." I\'m not sure yet, but I\'ll keep this in mind.\n\nOption B: Careful analysis of a specific variation can reveal previous themes of which it is a variation.\n\nThe passage mentions that every new idea is a variation of previous themes. So, if we take a specific variation, like a new discovery, careful analysis should show that it\'s based on older ideas. This seems to directly align with the passage, so this must be true.\n\nOption C: Some new scientific discoveries do not represent, on a deep level, a variation on previous themes.\n\nWait, the passage says that every new idea is some sort of variation. So, it\'s not saying that some don\'t vary; it\'s saying all do. Therefore, this statement in option C is the opposite of what the passage says. So, this must be false. But the question is asking which one must be true except for one, so this would be the exception.\n\nOption D: No scientific idea is entirely independent of all other ideas.\n\nThe passage implies that every idea is built upon previous ones, so none are entirely independent. This seems to be a direct restatement of the passage, so it must be true.\n\nSo, putting it all together:\n\n- A: Maybe not necessarily true because the passage doesn\'t specify only great discoverers.\n- B: Must be true.\n- C: Must be false because the passage says all are variations.\n- D: Must be true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is C because it\'s the only one that doesn\'t have to be true based on the passage.\n'] 90%|████████▉ | 537/600 [6:06:12<06:39,  6.34s/it]
