nly skin deep. This suggests that what seems beautiful is not really there, maybe just on the surface. The context says that mountains are beautiful after rain, even if no one appreciates it. So, it\'s about beauty existing without appreciation. This doesn\'t directly address whether beauty is superficial or not. So maybe not A.\n\nB) The taste is uncountable. This is about taste, not beauty. The context is about mountains and rain, so this seems unrelated. Maybe B is not the answer.\n\nC) Beauty is only in the eyes of people. The context is saying that beauty exists even if no one appreciates it, meaning beauty isn\'t dependent on people\'s eyes. So this statement is refuted because the text argues against the idea that beauty is only perceived by people. So C might be the answer.\n\nD) People just want to see what they like. This is more about wanting to see things they like, not about beauty. The context doesn\'t directly address this. So maybe D isn\'t the answer.\n\nWait, let me think again. The context is about mountains being beautiful after rain, but not being appreciated. So it\'s saying that beauty can exist without appreciation. So option C says beauty is only in the eyes of people, which would mean that if no one appreciates it, it\'s not beautiful. But the context is saying that it is beautiful even if no one appreciates it. So C is refuted because the text argues against the idea that beauty requires appreciation from people.\n\nSo the answer should be C.\n']
