      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to solve this logical reasoning problem. Let me read through the context and the question carefully.\n\nThe context says that most of the Earth's surface is covered by oceans. But studying the sea floor requires more sophisticated instruments, which means scientists know less about the undersea environment than any other part of Earth. So the main point here is that the sea floor is mostly hidden from view, and without advanced tools, we don't know much about it.\n\nThe question is asking which of the options, if true, best supports the discussion. The options are A, B, C, D. I need to figure out which one aligns with the idea that the sea floor is less understood because it's mostly hidden and hard to study.\n\nLet me go through each option one by one.\n\nOption A: Many of the mountains are completely below sea level, but the new underwater detection equipment produces as accurate images as the ones on land. Hmm, this is interesting. It says that even though the sea floor is under sea level, the equipment is just as accurate as on land. I'm not sure how this supports the idea that scientists know less. It might actually suggest that we can study the sea floor as well as mountains, which would contradict the context. So maybe A isn't the right choice.\n\nOption B: Powerful currents on the ocean floor are hard to understand compared to air currents on land. Well, this is talking about the complexity of ocean currents. While this is true, it doesn't directly relate to why we know less about the sea floor. The context is more about the accessibility of the sea floor rather than the movement of currents. So B might not be the best support.\n\nOption C: Sea-level temperature is stable because sunlight can't penetrate deep. This seems to be talking about environmental conditions. If the temperature is stable, maybe it's easier to study? Or maybe it's a factor in why we can't see or study deeper parts. I'm not sure how this connects to the main point about scientists knowing less. It might be more about the environment rather than the accessibility of the sea floor.\n\nOption D: Very few people have seen detailed sea floor maps, even though they're available in libraries. This directly supports the idea that the sea floor is less understood because it's not commonly accessible. If few people have seen the maps, then it's harder for scientists to study extensively. This seems to align well with the context given.\n\nSo, putting it all together, the best support would be option D because it highlights the lack of accessibility to the sea floor, which makes it harder for scientists to know more about it.\n</think>\n\nThe correct answer is D.\n\n**Answer: D) Very few people have seen detailed maps of sea floor extensions, even though such maps can be seen in almost every major library.**\n\n**Explanation:**\nOption D best supports the context by emphasizing the limited accessibility of sea floor maps, which makes it difficult for scientists to study and understand the undersea environment. This aligns with the idea that the sea floor is less understood due to its hidden nature.",
