      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to figure out which of the options A, B, C, or D represents the self depletion phenomenon based on the given context. Let me start by understanding what self depletion is. The context says that self depletion is when a person's ability or willingness to do voluntary activities declines over time due to long-term work. This includes things like losing the ability to control their environment, themselves, making choices, or starting behaviors. It also mentions that this leads to job burnout and self depletion without progress.\n\nSo, self depletion is about a gradual decline in motivation and ability over time, often because of prolonged work. The person might not make progress despite their efforts, and they might feel demotivated or stuck.\n\nNow, looking at the options:\n\nOption A: Lee didn't finish his thesis and kept putting it off, so he never graduated for eight years. This seems like a long-term struggle with a specific task, but does it fit the self depletion definition? It's more about not completing an important task rather than a general decline in ability or motivation over time.\n\nOption B: Chen has been a deputy director for 20 years and has published some personal works but hasn't been promoted. This shows a long-term career stagnation and lack of promotion, which could be related to self depletion because the person is not moving up despite their work, indicating a decline in motivation or opportunities.\n\nOption C: Song has been a salesperson for ten years, changed companies a few times, but his income hasn't changed because his performance was average. This is about job changes and not achieving better income despite trying. It might indicate some level of burnout, but it's more about the quality of work rather than a general decline in ability or motivation.\n\nOption D: Wang has worked as an archive administrator for 15 years and feels numb to his work, has no desire to change the status quo or get promoted. This seems to fit the definition because he's been working a long time, feels demotivated, and hasn't moved up, which aligns with self depletion where the ability or willingness to do voluntary work (like seeking a promotion) has declined.\n\nSo, comparing the options, D seems to best match the self depletion phenomenon because it's about a sustained decline in willingness to change roles or take on new challenges, leading to no progress despite continued work.\n</think>\n\nThe self depletion phenomenon is best represented by option D. Wang has been working for 15 years and feels demotivated, showing a decline in willingness to seek progress or change, which aligns with the definition.\n\n**Answer: D**",
