hings. This seems to fit because it's about the specific attribute (color) being misperceived.\n\nWait, but the definition says the patient is correct as an objective whole but inconsistent with individual attributes. So, in D, Sun's perception is correct in the sense that he can't see colors, but his ability is flawed because he can't perceive the color attributes correctly. So, his perception is inconsistent with the actual color attributes.\n\nSimilarly, in A, Wang's perception is of a fight scene where he's not actually seeing a real fight. So, his perception is inconsistent with the real attributes of the scene.\n\nSo, both A and D seem to fit. But let me think again.\n\nThe definition says the patient is correct as an objective whole. So, in A, Wang is correct in the sense that he's experiencing a fight scene, but the reality is different. So, his perception is inconsistent with the attributes (like the actual fight isn't happening). Similarly, in D, Sun's perception is correct in that he can't see colors, but his ability to perceive colors is flawed, making his perception inconsistent with the actual color attributes.\n\nBut the question is asking which of the following belongs to disturbance of perceptive synthesis. So, both A and D seem to fit, but I need to check the options again.\n\nWait, the options are:\n\nA) Wang's hallucinations about the game's fight scene.\n\nB) Zhang's perception of the pendulum stopping.\n\nC) Li's misidentification due to myopia.\n\nD) Sun's poor color distinction.\n\nSo, the definition is about perceiving a real thing but missing some attributes. In A, the fight scene is a hallucination, so it's a real thing (the game) but perceived incorrectly in terms of the fight details. In D, Sun can't perceive colors, so his perception is inconsistent with the color attributes.\n\nBut the definition says the patient is correct as an objective whole. So, in A, Wang is perceiving a fight scene, which is correct in the sense that it's a real