ld mean both are outstanding, which contradicts the class leader\'s statement if the leader was saying only one is outstanding. But if the leader was saying at least one, then A would be a possible outcome, but the party secretary is disagreeing, so maybe the party secretary thinks that neither is outstanding.\n\nOption B: Neither Li Xue nor Wang Lei can be rated as outstanding graduates. That would mean both are not outstanding, which would be the opposite of the class leader\'s statement if the leader was saying at least one is outstanding.\n\nOption C: Either both are rated as outstanding or neither is. That\'s an exclusive OR, meaning exactly one is outstanding, but that\'s not what C says. C says either both or neither, which is actually an exclusive OR in terms of the options, but in logic, it\'s more like a biconditional. Wait, no, if it\'s either both or neither, that\'s a biconditional, meaning they are equivalent. So if Li Xue is outstanding, then Wang Lei is, and vice versa. But that\'s not what the class leader said. The class leader said only one of them is outstanding, not both or neither.\n\nOption D: Li Xue was rated as an outstanding graduate, Wang Lei cannot be rated as an outstanding graduate. That would mean only Li Xue is outstanding, which aligns with the class leader\'s statement if it\'s an exclusive OR.\n\nBut the party secretary is disagreeing with the class leader. So the party secretary\'s opinion is the opposite of the class leader\'s statement.\n\nIf the class leader said "either A or B," the party secretary would say "neither A nor B," which is option B. Alternatively, if the class leader\'s statement was an exclusive OR, the party secretary could be saying that both are not, which is option B.\n\nWait, but let\'s clarify. The class leader\'s statement is "either Li Xue or Wang Lei is outstanding." If that\'s an exclusive OR, meaning exactly one, then the party secretary disagreeing would mean that neither is outstanding, which is option B.\n\nAlternatively, if the class leader\'s statement was inclusive OR (at least one), then the party secretary disagreeing would mean that neither is outstanding, which is still option B.\n\nSo regardless of whether the OR is exclusive or inclusive, the party secretary\'s opinion is that neither is outstanding, which is option B.\n\nWait, but let me think again. If the class leader said "either Li Xue or Wang Lei is outstanding," and the party secretary disagrees, then the party secretary must be saying that it\'s not the case that either is outstanding. So the negation of "either Li Xue or Wang Lei" is "neither Li Xue nor Wang Lei." So the party secretary\'s opinion is that neither is outstanding, which is option B.\n\nYes, that makes sense. So the correct answer should be B.\n'] 98%|█████████▊| 586/600 [7:02:32<11:54, 51.01s/it]
